window.BOLLINGER_MANUSCRIPTS = {"1.1":{"title":"What Are the Bands Showing You?","timeLabel":"15 minutes","take":"The bands help you describe where price is relative to recent behaviour. The market context helps you decide what that observation might mean.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 1.1</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>Why do the bands move closer to price in some periods and spread out in others?</p>\n<p>A Bollinger Band chart adds three moving lines around price. The middle line is usually a 20-period simple moving average. It gives a moving reference point based on recent prices. The upper and lower bands sit above and below that average. Their distance from the middle line changes with how much price has been moving around its average.</p>\n<p>Think of the bands as a frame around recent price behaviour. The frame adjusts as conditions change. It is not a pair of permanent walls.</p>\n<h4>Read what is visible first</h4>\n<p>In Figure 1.1, find the three parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Middle band:</strong> the moving average used as the centre of the calculation.</li>\n<li><strong>Upper band:</strong> the middle band plus a chosen amount based on recent price variation.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower band:</strong> the middle band minus the same amount.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The standard setup commonly uses 20 periods for the middle average and two standard deviations for the outer bands. You do not need the formula yet. For now, remember the logic: the average gives the centre; the amount price has varied helps determine how far the outer bands sit from it.</p>\n<h4>What the bands add to a candle chart</h4>\n<p>A candle tells you where price opened, how far it travelled during that period, and where it closed. The bands add a moving reference for judging where that price sits relative to recent conditions.</p>\n<p>Suppose price reaches the upper band. You now know it has reached the upper part of the banded range. You do <strong>not</strong> yet know whether it will reverse. In a sideways market, it might be approaching a range edge. In a strong uptrend, it might be showing continuing strength. The band gives you a useful question, not a complete answer.</p>\n<h4>A simple reading method</h4>\n<p>When a band interaction catches your eye, ask three things:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Where is price?</strong> Is it near the upper band, middle band, or lower band?</li>\n<li><strong>What has the market been doing?</strong> Is price trending, moving sideways, or changing character?</li>\n<li><strong>What would help next?</strong> Does price hold its progress, turn back, or continue moving in the same direction?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These questions prevent a common mistake: treating the indicator as if it had one meaning in every market.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p><strong>Mistake:</strong> “The band is resistance, so price should turn.”</p>\n<p><strong>Correction:</strong> The upper and lower bands describe relative price position. They do not guarantee that price will stop or reverse.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The bands help you describe where price is relative to recent behaviour. The market context helps you decide what that observation might mean.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> This mental model follows John Bollinger&#39;s official rules, especially Rules 1, 6, 7 and 22, and the construction discussion in <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>. <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Worked chart comparison: Read the frame as it changes</h4>\n<p><strong>Case A — Quiet movement.</strong> Imagine the middle band is near 100, the upper band near 104 and the lower band near 96. Several candles fluctuate between 98 and 102 and the outer bands gradually draw closer. The defensible observation is that the recent distribution of prices is becoming tighter. It does not tell you that price is about to rise, fall, or break out immediately.</p>\n<p><strong>Case B — Movement expands.</strong> Later, closes spread from around 100 toward 106 and the band gap widens. The widening reflects the larger recent variation, but the direction must still come from the candles and their sequence. If price keeps making higher highs and higher lows, that supports an upward reading; if it falls through a range floor, it supports a different one. The band shape is useful precisely because it describes the change without deciding the trade for you.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe: Start with what you can see</h4>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> Using Figure 1.1, point to one section where the bands are relatively narrow and one where they are wider. Identify the middle, upper, and lower lines.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The three lines can be identified by their positions around price. If the distance between the outer bands changes, the indicator is responding to a change in recent price variation. That observation alone does not identify the next direction.</p>\n<h4>Interpret: What might the evidence mean?</h4>\n<p>Imagine two charts with price at the upper band. In Chart A, price has been moving back and forth between fairly stable boundaries. In Chart B, price has been making higher highs and higher lows for several periods. The same visible band touch sits in two different market stories. Chart A may deserve a closer look for a range-edge reaction; Chart B may be showing trend strength.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> In one or two sentences, explain why the touch has a different possible meaning in the two charts.</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> In the range, the touch is near a boundary where price has turned before, so a reaction is worth watching. In the rising trend, repeated upper-band interaction may reflect ongoing strength. Neither chart gives an entry from the touch alone.</p>","quiz":{"question":"The middle band is near 100 and the outer bands move from 96/104 to 94/106. What is the strongest reading?","choices":["Recent price variation has widened; the bands alone do not choose direction.","Price is guaranteed to rise because the upper band moved higher.","The new bands are fixed support and resistance."],"correct":0,"why":"The wider gap is consistent with greater variation in the recent calculation window. Price structure is still needed to judge direction."}},"1.2":{"title":"When Price Reaches a Band, What Does It Mean?","timeLabel":"14 minutes","take":"A band touch is a reason to inspect the chart, not a complete trading plan.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 1.2</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>If price touches the upper band, should you sell?</p>\n<p>No. John Bollinger makes the distinction very clearly: a band tag is not, by itself, a signal. The band gives a relative definition of high or low. It does not tell you to buy or sell automatically.</p>\n<h4>One visible event, two different contexts</h4>\n<p>Start with a market that has moved sideways. Price has repeatedly risen toward an area and then fallen back. If it now reaches the upper band close to that area, it is reasonable to watch for another rejection. But even there, you still need evidence that price is turning. The band and the range boundary identify a place to pay attention; they do not guarantee a reversal.</p>\n<p>Now consider a market that is steadily rising. Price forms higher swing highs and higher swing lows, and several candles close near the upper band. Selling each upper-band touch would mean repeatedly trading against the direction that price is showing. In this setting, the band interaction may fit continuation better than reversal.</p>\n<p>The point is not that range trades always reverse or trend trades always continue. The point is that <strong>the same indicator event can play a different role depending on the market around it</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Observation is not a conclusion</h4>\n<p>Use the following language when describing a chart:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Observation:</strong> Price reached the upper band.</li>\n<li><strong>Possible interpretation in a range:</strong> Price is testing the upper part of the recent range.</li>\n<li><strong>Alternative:</strong> The range may be breaking, or the touch may be an ordinary movement within the range.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence to watch:</strong> Does price turn back into the range, or does it hold above the boundary and continue?</li>\n<li><strong>Decision:</strong> Wait until the condition required by your plan appears.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That may sound slower than instantly labelling a buy or sell signal. It is also more honest about what the chart has actually shown.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Treating “upper” as sell and “lower” as buy.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>A band touch is a reason to inspect the chart, not a complete trading plan.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger&#39;s Official Rule 6 says tags of the bands are just that—tags, not signals. Rules 7 and 22 reinforce the need for context and setup-based decisions. <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Read the comparison</h4>\n<p>In the range panel, look for earlier turns near the same area. In the trend panel, follow the swing structure and the sequence of interactions with the upper band. Do not count band touches in isolation. Look at what price did before and after them.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: The same touch, two different readings</h4>\n<p><strong>Case A — Range edge.</strong> A market has turned lower from roughly 104 on several earlier attempts. Price now reaches 104 while the upper band is nearby, then closes back at 102. The prior turns make a rejection worth investigating. The close back below the area adds evidence that price has not held above it, but the trader still needs the entry rule and invalidation used for the range plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Case B — Advancing market.</strong> Another market rises from 100 to 103, then 106 and 109. Several closes sit near the upper band while swing lows also rise. Selling simply because the upper band was touched would ignore the persistent progress. The contrast is not that range tags always reverse and trends always continue. It is that you should first compare the touch with structure, then wait for the condition your plan requires.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: What does the touch tell you?</h4>\n<p>For each panel, finish this sentence: “The band touch tells me ___, but it does not yet tell me ___.”</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> For the range, the touch shows price is near the upper side of its recent banded range; it does not prove that the range will hold or that a short trade is ready. For the trend, the touch shows price is at a relatively high position; it does not prove price is overextended or due to fall.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>The lower band is touched after several candles have made lower lows. A learner says, “This is cheap, so I will buy.”</p>\n<p>What is missing from that reasoning?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> The learner has described relative position as if it were value and a reversal signal. The chart may be in a downtrend, with price walking the lower band. The learner needs evidence that the decline is weakening, a clear trigger, and a planned invalidation before considering a long.</p>","quiz":{"question":"Price touches the upper band in a rising sequence of higher highs and higher lows. What does the touch establish?","choices":["A short entry is due because price is high.","The trend must end on this candle.","Price is near the upper side of the current frame; context and a separate trigger are still needed."],"correct":2,"why":"A band tag is not a standalone signal. In a directional sequence, repeated upper-band contact can accompany strength."}},"1.3":{"title":"Why the Bands Keep Changing","timeLabel":"15 minutes","take":"Narrower bands mean recent price movement has been quieter; wider bands mean it has been broader. Neither state chooses a direction for you.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 1.3</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>Why can an instrument show narrow bands one week and much wider bands another week?</p>\n<p>The bands adjust to recent price variation. When price moves in a quieter, more contained way, the outer bands tend to move closer together. When price begins making larger moves, the bands tend to spread apart. Traders often call this changing level of movement <strong>volatility</strong>.</p>\n<p>Volatility is not the same as direction. A market can become more volatile while rising, falling, or moving sharply in both directions. The bands show that the recent movement has changed; you still need price structure to judge the direction.</p>\n<h4>Think of the bands as an adjustable frame</h4>\n<p>Imagine wrapping a flexible frame around a sequence of prices. If recent prices are close to their average, the frame can be narrow. If prices have been spread farther from their average, the frame expands. Bollinger Bands work from a moving average and a measure of how spread out recent prices have been.</p>\n<p>This also means the bands can change even if the current candle does not look dramatic. The calculation uses a rolling group of prices. New bars enter the calculation and older bars leave it. The band shape reflects the selected window, not just the last candle.</p>\n<h4>Three states to notice</h4>\n<p>Figure 1.3 presents three common states:</p>\n<p><strong>Narrow bands:</strong> Recent price movement has been relatively quiet compared with the wider parts of the chart. This can be useful context, but it does not tell you when a move will happen or which way it will go.</p>\n<p><strong>Bands beginning to widen:</strong> Recent price variation is increasing. A larger move may be underway, but widening bands alone do not say whether it is a clean breakout, a sharp reversal, or a period of choppy swings.</p>\n<p><strong>Wide bands:</strong> Price has recently been moving more broadly. A new entry may now be late if the move has already travelled far from the level that would make the setup attractive.</p>\n<p>The same band width can also mean different things across instruments or timeframes. A width that is unusually narrow for one market may be ordinary for another. We will later use BandWidth to make relative comparisons more systematic.</p>\n<h4>What widening does—and does not—confirm</h4>\n<p>Suppose price breaks above a range and the bands widen. That pairing is consistent with stronger movement after a quiet period. It is not enough by itself to prove that the breakout will continue. A fast move that immediately fails may also widen the bands.</p>\n<p>Read the price result alongside the width change. Did price move beyond a boundary? Did it hold there? Are the swing highs and lows supporting the same direction? If the evidence conflicts, the correct response may be to wait.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Assuming narrow bands must be followed immediately by a big move, or assuming wide bands must soon contract.</p>\n<p><strong>Correction:</strong> Bands describe the recent calculation window. They do not provide a timer or a direction forecast.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Narrower bands mean recent price movement has been quieter; wider bands mean it has been broader. Neither state chooses a direction for you.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> This follows the construction and volatility-cycle discussion in <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em> and official Rules 9, 18 and 19. A secondary, accessible explanation is available from <a href=\"https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-overlays/bollinger-bands\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">StockCharts</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Worked comparison: Compression is not a countdown</h4>\n<p><strong>Case A — Width contracts.</strong> BandWidth on an instrument falls from a typical 6% reading to about 2.5% while candles overlap inside a compact range. You can describe the market as quieter relative to its own recent history. Mark the range high and low and prepare conditional scenarios. The low reading does not say when the quiet period will end.</p>\n<p><strong>Case B — Width expands.</strong> Price then closes above the range and the bands spread. This is consistent with a move becoming broader, but the first break can still fail. If price quickly returns inside, the expansion may have reflected the failed move rather than durable continuation. A useful reading therefore combines width with the price result after the change. Compare similar conditions across different periods instead of treating one BandWidth number as a universal threshold.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Match width to price movement</h4>\n<p>Use Figure 1.3 to match the quiet, expanding, and wide-band sections to their visible price behaviour. Then name one conclusion that cannot be drawn from band width alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The narrower section shows less recent variation, the widening section shows variation increasing, and the wide section shows broader recent movement. Band width alone cannot identify the next direction or guarantee a breakout.</p>\n<h4>Test: What evidence would you wait for next?</h4>\n<p>A second chart shows narrow bands, followed by a sudden increase in candle size. Before choosing a direction, name one price event you would watch for and one condition that would make you reject the first move.</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> I would first identify a meaningful nearby boundary and watch whether price closes beyond it and meets the rule chosen for the setup. A quick return into the prior range may weaken the first-break idea. Wider bands alone do not choose the direction.</p>","quiz":{"question":"BandWidth has reached a low reading for this instrument. What can you conclude?","choices":["A large upward move must begin next.","The bands are unusually narrow relative to the chosen history; direction and timing remain unknown.","The market is too quiet to move lower."],"correct":1,"why":"Compression describes recent variation. It does not provide a countdown or a directional forecast."}},"1.4":{"title":"What the Bands Cannot Tell You","timeLabel":"13 minutes","take":"Bollinger Bands describe relative price and volatility. They do not remove uncertainty, and they do not replace a trading plan.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 1.4</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>Can the bands tell you the next move or guarantee that price will stay between them?</p>\n<p>No. The bands are useful reference points, not a fence around future prices. Price can touch a band, close outside it, keep moving, return inside, or move sideways. None of those outcomes is ruled out just because the bands are on the chart.</p>\n<h4>Why the statistical language can mislead</h4>\n<p>The outer bands are commonly set two standard deviations away from a moving average. It is tempting to assume that almost all price observations should remain inside them. But financial prices do not reliably behave like a neat, stable bell-shaped distribution. Bollinger notes that with the default parameters the proportion inside the bands is typically closer to 90% than 95%.</p>\n<p>The practical lesson matters more than the number: do not treat a band as a statistical barrier that price is not supposed to cross. A close outside the bands is an event to interpret with context.</p>\n<h4>A close outside a band is not automatically a reversal</h4>\n<p>Bollinger&#39;s rule is counterintuitive to many new users: a close outside the bands is initially treated as a continuation signal, not a reversal signal. This does not mean every outside close should be traded. It means the close itself is not evidence that price must turn back.</p>\n<p>Compare the two outside-close examples in Figure 1.4. In one, price continues in the direction of the move. In the other, price returns inside and the breakout fails. At the moment of the first close, you do not know which future path will develop. A trading plan needs a defined trigger, risk, and invalidation; it should not depend on guessing the outcome.</p>\n<h4>Use the bands to ask better questions</h4>\n<p>The bands can help you ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is price at a relatively high or low position compared with its recent movement?</li>\n<li>Has recent price variation become unusually narrow or broad?</li>\n<li>Is price moving persistently along one band?</li>\n<li>Does the current movement fit the market structure, or is the structure changing?</li>\n<li>What would make my current explanation less convincing?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They cannot answer on their own:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Who is buying or selling, or why.</li>\n<li>Whether a move will continue or reverse with certainty.</li>\n<li>The right position size for your account.</li>\n<li>Whether the trade has enough room to a target.</li>\n<li>Whether a setup has a positive result across a range of historical cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Using the bands as a prediction device.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Bollinger Bands describe relative price and volatility. They do not remove uncertainty, and they do not replace a trading plan.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger&#39;s official Rules 7, 8, 14 and 22, and the book&#39;s chapter on statistics. <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Worked comparison: What was knowable at the close?</h4>\n<p><strong>Case A — Continuation.</strong> At the end of a candle, price closes above an upper band near 110. The next two candles close around 112 and 114. In hindsight, continuation is visible. At the first close, however, the only justified statement was that price had closed outside the band; the future candles were not yet known.</p>\n<p><strong>Case B — Failure.</strong> A different chart also closes above 110, then returns below the breakout area and falls back into the prior range. That close eventually becomes part of a failed move. The initial candle looked similar in both cases. This is why a decision should be judged using only the evidence available at that time, and why a setup needs a pre-written trigger and invalidation rather than a prediction that the first move will succeed.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Separate facts from claims</h4>\n<p>For each of the four panels in Figure 1.4, write one supported statement and one unsupported conclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> “Price closed outside the upper band” is supported by the chart. “Price must reverse next” is not. “Price closed outside and later continued” is supported after the later bars appear, but it was not certain at the first close. Keep the decision-time evidence separate from hindsight.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>A chart shows a close below the lower band after several falling candles. What can you say without assuming a trade?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> Price has closed below the lower band during recent weakness. The move may continue, or it may fail and return inside. I need to judge the trend, nearby structure, and my chosen trigger and invalidation before planning a trade.</p>","quiz":{"question":"A completed candle closes above the upper band. What is the most careful first reading?","choices":["The close may be consistent with continuation; it does not prove continuation or reversal.","Price must return to the middle band immediately.","The close confirms that the instrument is statistically overbought."],"correct":0,"why":"The close outside the band is an observation. The later path is unknown at that decision point."}},"2.1":{"title":"How the Bands Are Built","timeLabel":"16 minutes","take":"The middle band gives the moving average; standard deviation gives the outer bands their changing distance.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 2.1</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>What changes when recent prices are spread farther from their average?</p>\n<p>Bollinger Bands have a centre and two outer bands. The centre is usually a 20-period simple moving average. The word <em>period</em> means the chart bars included in the calculation: 20 daily bars on a daily chart, or 20 five-minute bars on a five-minute chart.</p>\n<p>The outer bands are calculated by adding and subtracting a chosen number of standard deviations from the middle average. You do not need to calculate standard deviation by hand to use the indicator. Think of it as a way to measure how widely recent prices have varied around their average.</p>\n<h4>Build the picture in three steps</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Find the average.</strong> The middle band smooths recent prices, making it easier to see a moving reference line rather than focus on every small movement.</li>\n<li><strong>Measure how spread out price has been.</strong> When recent prices have stayed close to their average, the spread is smaller. When they have moved more widely around it, the spread is larger.</li>\n<li><strong>Place the bands around the average.</strong> The upper band sits above the average and the lower band sits below it. The selected spread measure determines their distance.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The common default is a 20-period simple moving average with the outer bands set two standard deviations away. The result is a band whose width adapts to recent price variation instead of staying a fixed percentage away from the average.</p>\n<h4>Why the difference matters</h4>\n<p>Compare Bollinger Bands with a fixed-percentage envelope. A fixed envelope stays at a chosen percentage above and below its average, even when recent price movement becomes much quieter or much larger. Bollinger Bands adapt their width to recent variation. That is what makes them useful for comparing changing volatility.</p>\n<p>The trade-off is that the band depends on its settings and calculation window. It is not an objective boundary independent of choices. If you change the moving-average period or the standard-deviation multiplier, you change the view.</p>\n<h4>A useful limitation</h4>\n<p>The bands are often described in statistical language, but price movements are not guaranteed to follow a normal distribution. Do not assume that two standard deviations means the same fixed percentage of price bars will always stay inside the bands. It is a calculation setting, not a promise about what the market will do.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Thinking the bands are a fixed percentage around price.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The middle band gives the moving average; standard deviation gives the outer bands their changing distance.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger&#39;s official Rules 9 and 14 and the Construction and Statistics chapters of <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>. <a href=\"https://www.mheducation.com/highered/mhp/product/bollinger-bollinger-bands\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Publisher contents</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Read Figure 2.1</h4>\n<p>Use the diagram to identify which element controls the centre and which element controls the outer-band distance.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> Complete these statements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The middle band is based on a ___.</li>\n<li>The outer bands are placed using a measure of recent price ___.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The middle band is based on a moving average. The outer bands use standard deviation to measure how spread out recent prices have been. The settings determine the calculation.</p>\n<h4>Worked calculation: See what the numbers mean</h4>\n<p>Suppose the selected moving average is 100 and the standard deviation for the chosen window is 2. With a multiplier of 2, the upper band is 104 and the lower band is 96. If price values become more spread out and the calculated standard deviation rises to 3 while the average remains near 100, the bands move to about 106 and 94. The middle line can move too, because it is recalculated as new periods enter and older ones leave the window.</p>\n<p>The lesson is not to calculate every band by hand. It is to understand which input changed. A widening band gap may come from more variable prices; a shifting middle line reflects a changing average. If you compare two screenshots, first verify that they use the same instrument, timeframe, price input, period and multiplier. Otherwise you may be comparing two different calculations rather than a true change in market conditions.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>Recent candles become larger and the prices move farther away from their average. Without changing any settings, what would you generally expect the bands to do?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> The bands would generally widen as the calculation reflects greater recent variation. The wider bands would describe the change in volatility; they would not tell me whether to buy or sell.</p>","quiz":{"question":"With a middle average of 100, a standard deviation of 2 and a multiplier of 2, where are the outer bands?","choices":["98 and 102.","96 and 104.","90 and 110."],"correct":1,"why":"Two deviations of 2 give a distance of 4 from the middle average: 104 above and 96 below."}},"2.2":{"title":"Why 20 and 2 Are Defaults, Not Laws","timeLabel":"14 minutes","take":"Choose settings for a clear reason, then test them consistently. Do not let hindsight choose them for you.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 2.2</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>Is a chart better because its indicator settings make the past look neat?</p>\n<p>Not necessarily. A familiar starting point is a 20-period simple moving average and bands two standard deviations away. Bollinger presents these as defaults, not universal laws. Settings affect how quickly the indicator reacts and how much smoothing it provides.</p>\n<h4>What happens when you change the settings?</h4>\n<p>A shorter averaging period reacts more quickly to recent price changes. It may also produce a more restless middle line and more frequent changes in the bands. A longer period usually looks smoother, but it can react more slowly when conditions change.</p>\n<p>The standard-deviation multiplier affects the distance between the middle line and outer bands. A larger multiplier places the outer bands farther away; a smaller multiplier brings them closer. These changes affect how often price reaches or closes outside the bands.</p>\n<p>The settings are linked. If you change the averaging period, the level of variation used for the outer bands also changes. Bollinger suggests modest adjustments when the averaging period changes, rather than treating the two inputs as completely unrelated. His examples include a 50-period average with 2.1 deviations and a 10-period average with 1.9 deviations. These are examples of coordinated settings, not proof that either is best for your instrument.</p>\n<h4>Faster is not automatically better</h4>\n<p>Figure 2.2 shows the same price history with different settings. Do not ask which version produces the most impressive historical signal. Ask what each version makes easier or harder to see.</p>\n<p>A fast setting may identify a change earlier, but it can also react to ordinary noise. A slower setting can reduce small fluctuations, but it may show a change later. The choice depends on your trading question, timeframe, instrument, and the way you will test the resulting rules.</p>\n<h4>Avoid hindsight tuning</h4>\n<p>A common trap is to move settings until they fit a trade you wish you had taken. You may end up designing a setting that describes that particular historical section exceptionally well but does not transfer to other periods.</p>\n<p>A fair test keeps the rules stable while you review a varied sample. If you decide to test a new setting, treat it as a new version of the method and compare it against the old one under the same conditions. Do not keep changing parameters after every loss.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Treating the default as magic—or assuming a custom setting is superior because it looks cleaner.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Choose settings for a clear reason, then test them consistently. Do not let hindsight choose them for you.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger&#39;s official Rules 9, 11 and 12. His suggested adjustments are examples, not universal recommendations. <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Worked comparison: Faster is not automatically more useful</h4>\n<p>Imagine the same price chart has a sharp two-candle jump followed by a quiet pullback. A 10-period setting will generally react to the new bars faster than a 20-period setting. That can make the bands turn or widen sooner, but it can also make them respond more often to ordinary short-term noise. A 50-period setting will usually smooth the sequence and react more slowly, which may help with a longer view but can delay a short-term reading.</p>\n<p>None of these settings is automatically correct. Choose settings to match a clearly stated purpose, then keep them fixed while reviewing a varied set of examples. If the only reason for changing a period is that a different value made one past trade look perfect, you are fitting the tool to the outcome. Compare one change at a time and record what becomes easier to see—and what becomes harder.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe: Read how the bands are built</h4>\n<p>Look at the three versions in Figure 2.2. Which reacts most quickly to a sudden price change? Which looks smoother? Does either picture prove which setting will perform better in the future?</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The shorter period should react more quickly; the longer period should look smoother. The picture alone cannot prove future performance. That requires consistent testing across different market conditions.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>You change the settings after every losing trade, and each new version looks better on the examples you just reviewed. What is the problem?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> I am repeatedly fitting the settings to past outcomes. The result may describe the sample rather than produce a repeatable method. I should choose rules deliberately, test them on a varied set, and evaluate a new version separately.</p>","quiz":{"question":"Why should you avoid changing the period after every disappointing example?","choices":["Because a longer period always produces better trades.","Because settings cannot affect band shape.","Because you risk fitting the indicator to past outcomes instead of testing a stable rule."],"correct":2,"why":"Keep the rule stable while comparing varied cases. Change one input at a time and test the change beyond the examples used to invent it."}},"2.3":{"title":"Use %B to Describe Price Position","timeLabel":"15 minutes","take":"%B describes where price is relative to the bands. The market context decides what that location may mean.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 2.3</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>How can you describe the location of price more precisely than saying it is “near the top” or “near the bottom”?</p>\n<p>%B, usually read as “percent B”, turns the price&#39;s location relative to the Bollinger Bands into a number. It is calculated from price&#39;s position between the lower and upper bands.</p>\n<p>The important reference points are easy to remember:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>%B = 0:</strong> price is at the lower band.</li>\n<li><strong>%B = 0.5:</strong> price is halfway between the lower and upper bands.</li>\n<li><strong>%B = 1:</strong> price is at the upper band.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 0:</strong> price is below the lower band.</li>\n<li><strong>Above 1:</strong> price is above the upper band.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some charting platforms display %B in a slightly different number format or use the indicator on a different price input. Check the platform&#39;s definition if you are comparing values from different tools.</p>\n<h4>What a %B value adds</h4>\n<p>Suppose two charts look different because one instrument trades at a much higher price than the other. Saying “it is close to the upper band” is a useful visual description, but a %B value can make relative position easier to compare. It removes the need to express band position in currency units.</p>\n<p>But %B does not tell you whether price is expensive, cheap, overbought, or oversold in a broad sense. It describes position within the current bands. A value above 1 means price is outside the upper band; it does not mean price must reverse. A value below 0 means price is outside the lower band; it does not mean a buy is due.</p>\n<h4>Same value, different market</h4>\n<p>Imagine %B reads 0.95. In one chart, price is near the upper band while moving sideways inside a well-established range. In another, price has been advancing and continues to form higher highs. The number describes a similar relative location, but the context changes the question you should ask.</p>\n<p>In the range, you might inspect whether the range edge is holding and whether price shows a turn. In the trend, you might inspect whether the movement continues to hold its progress. The reading is not a trade signal by itself.</p>\n<h4>Use %B to organise evidence</h4>\n<p>Bollinger also discusses using %B in pattern recognition and indicator comparison. If you use it to look for divergence—where price makes a new high or low but the indicator does not show the same behaviour—treat the mismatch as a reason to investigate, not a guarantee of reversal. Divergences can persist while a trend continues.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Treating %B above 1 as a sell instruction or below 0 as a buy instruction.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>%B describes where price is relative to the bands. The market context decides what that location may mean.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger&#39;s official Rules 15–17 and the %B discussion in <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>. A secondary formula reference is <a href=\"https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-indicators/b-indicator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">StockCharts %B</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Worked calculation: Turn %B into a description</h4>\n<p>Suppose the lower band is 90 and the upper band is 110. The band span is 20. If the close is 105, %B is (105 − 90) ÷ 20 = 0.75. Price is three-quarters of the way from the lower band toward the upper band. If the close is 110, %B is 1; if it closes at 112, %B is 1.1, meaning price is above the upper band.</p>\n<p>Now compare two charts with %B around 0.9. In one, price is progressing through higher highs and higher lows. In the other, a sharp rally has run into an established resistance area and the next candles fail to hold the high. The number describes a similar relative position; it does not make the two charts the same trade. Use %B to make the observation more precise, then interpret it with structure and the method you intend to test.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Compare the settings</h4>\n<p>Using Figure 2.3, match the readings 0, 0.5, 1, -0.2 and 1.2 to their positions.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> 0 is the lower band, 0.5 is halfway, and 1 is the upper band. -0.2 is below the lower band, and 1.2 is above the upper band.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>Price is above the upper band and %B is 1.15. Write one fact, one possible interpretation, and one thing you still need to know.</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> Fact: price is above the upper band and %B is above 1. Possible reading: price is moving strongly relative to its recent range. I still need to know whether the market is trending, whether price holds its progress, and what would invalidate a trade idea.</p>","quiz":{"question":"The lower band is 90, the upper band is 110 and the close is 105. What does %B = 0.75 describe?","choices":["Price is about three-quarters of the way from the lower to the upper band.","The next candle has a 75% chance of rising.","BandWidth is 75%."],"correct":0,"why":"%B measures relative price location inside or beyond the bands. It does not forecast the next candle."}},"2.4":{"title":"Use BandWidth to See When the Bands Are Narrow or Wide","timeLabel":"15 minutes","take":"BandWidth helps you see when the bands are unusually narrow or widening. It does not tell you which way to trade.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 2.4</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>How do you know whether today&#39;s bands are narrow compared with this instrument&#39;s own history?</p>\n<p>BandWidth measures the distance between the upper and lower bands relative to the middle band. In simple terms, it turns the width you can see into a value you can compare across time. The exact display format may differ by platform, so check whether your chart shows a decimal or a percentage.</p>\n<h4>What to look for</h4>\n<p>When BandWidth is low, the bands are narrow relative to price. When BandWidth rises, the bands are widening. A very low reading compared with the same instrument&#39;s recent history can help you identify a period of unusually quiet movement. This state is often called a <strong>Squeeze</strong> in Bollinger&#39;s work.</p>\n<p>The key word is <em>relative</em>. A BandWidth reading that is low for one instrument may be normal for another. Even on the same instrument, the useful comparison depends on the history and timeframe you are studying. For that reason, avoid adopting a universal BandWidth number and assuming it identifies every squeeze.</p>\n<h4>Width is not direction</h4>\n<p>BandWidth tells you about band width, not whether buyers or sellers will win the next move. A low reading can be followed by an upward break, a downward break, or an initial move that fails. When the bands begin to widen, volatility is increasing; that still does not prove that the current direction will last.</p>\n<p>Figure 2.4 aligns BandWidth with price so you can connect the measurement to the chart. The low area on the BandWidth line corresponds to a period when the price bands are close together. The rise in BandWidth corresponds to the bands widening. Check the price structure separately to understand what price is doing during those changes.</p>\n<h4>Why use a separate width measure?</h4>\n<p>On a crowded chart, it can be difficult to compare the width of today&#39;s bands with the width several months ago. A lower panel makes the change easier to see. It also encourages you to compare the value with its own history rather than judging it from one glance.</p>\n<p>BandWidth is not a countdown clock. It does not say that a breakout must occur in the next bar or that a narrow period will resolve in a particular direction. It helps you recognise a condition worth monitoring.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Using one BandWidth value as a universal squeeze threshold across all markets.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>BandWidth helps you see when the bands are unusually narrow or widening. It does not tell you which way to trade.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger&#39;s official Rules 18–19 and the BandWidth and Squeeze chapters of <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>. See also <a href=\"https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-indicators/bollinger-bandwidth\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">StockCharts BandWidth</a>.</p>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Worked comparison: Is the width unusually small for this market?</h4>\n<p>Imagine a share normally has a BandWidth reading between 4% and 9%, but the current reading has fallen to 2.2%. That is unusually narrow relative to its own recent behaviour and may justify closer monitoring. Yet price is sitting in the middle of a broad, messy range with no clear boundary nearby. The correct next step is to mark the structure or wait—not to buy just because the width is low.</p>\n<p>A different instrument might normally trade with a much smaller percentage width. Comparing the raw width of the two instruments can be misleading. BandWidth is more informative when you compare the same instrument with its own history and use a stated lookback. Once width begins to rise, describe that expansion separately from the direction of price. The width measure tells you about the envelope; the candles and levels help you assess the move.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe: Match %B to price position</h4>\n<p>Use Figure 2.4 to identify the narrowest period shown, then find where BandWidth begins to rise. What changes in the price chart during that time?</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The low point in BandWidth corresponds to relatively narrow bands. When BandWidth rises, the outer bands spread farther apart. Price may also begin moving more broadly, but direction must be judged from the candles and structure.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>BandWidth is low, but price is in the centre of a messy range and no clear boundary is nearby. Should the low reading alone trigger a trade?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> No. It identifies quieter conditions, not an entry or direction. I should identify a meaningful price boundary, define what would count as a trigger, and decide in advance what would invalidate the plan.</p>","quiz":{"question":"BandWidth is unusually low but price is in the middle of a messy range. What is the best response?","choices":["Buy before the bands expand.","Wait for a meaningful boundary and a defined trigger; low width alone is not an entry.","Use the same absolute BandWidth threshold for every instrument."],"correct":1,"why":"The width reading identifies compression. A useful plan still needs a price level, a trigger and an invalidation."}},"3.1":{"title":"First Decide: Trend or Range?","timeLabel":"17 minutes","take":"Decide whether price is trending, ranging, or unclear before you decide what a band touch might mean.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 3.1</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>Why can the same lower-band touch be interesting in one chart and dangerous in another?</p>\n<p>Because the bands sit inside a market that already has some structure. Before you interpret the outer band, ask whether price has been making progress in one direction or rotating back and forth between boundaries.</p>\n<h4>A practical way to read context</h4>\n<p>A <strong>trend</strong> is a period in which price generally makes progress in one direction. In an uptrend, swing highs and swing lows tend to rise. In a downtrend, they tend to fall. Price does not have to move in a straight line; pullbacks happen inside trends.</p>\n<p>A <strong>range</strong> is a period in which price repeatedly moves between areas where it has turned or stalled. The boundaries may be clear or approximate, and no boundary is guaranteed to hold. A range is more than a short pause. You need enough visible back-and-forth behaviour to justify treating its edges as potential reference areas.</p>\n<p>Some charts are <strong>mixed or transitioning</strong>. Price may be leaving a range, losing trend structure, or moving in a way that does not yet support a confident label. Calling a chart “unclear” is not a failure. It is a way to avoid building a trade on a context you have not established.</p>\n<h4>Where the middle band helps</h4>\n<p>The middle band can help you describe whether price is spending more time above it or below it and whether it is generally rising, falling, or flattening. But do not turn this into a mechanical rule such as “above the middle band means buy.” In a range, price crosses the middle band repeatedly. During transitions, it may cross several times without resolving direction.</p>\n<p>Read the middle band alongside swing structure. If the middle band is rising and price is making higher swing highs and lows, those observations support an uptrend reading. If the middle band is flat and price keeps returning between boundaries, a range reading may fit better. Neither combination guarantees what happens next.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Calling every pause in a rising market a range and then selling the upper band.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Decide whether price is trending, ranging, or unclear before you decide what a band touch might mean.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> This is a course context-reading framework built around Bollinger&#39;s emphasis on price structure and his rule that bands do not give continuous advice. <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Inspect Figure 3.1</h4>\n<p>Panel A shows a rising trend. Panel B shows price rotating between a high and low area. Panel C is deliberately mixed. Do not force Panel C into a trend or range just because the task asks you to classify it; the “unclear” answer may be the best supported.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: Trend, range or not yet clear?</h4>\n<p><strong>Case A — Trend.</strong> Over a sequence of swings, the highs progress from 102 to 105 to 108 and the pullback lows rise from 98 to 101 to 104. The middle band also slopes upward. Together, those facts support a rising-trend reading. An upper-band touch in this sequence should not automatically be treated as a short.</p>\n<p><strong>Case B — Range or transition.</strong> Another chart has repeated turns around 104 and 98, with price crossing the middle band several times. That is more consistent with a range. If the next candle suddenly closes at 106, the context does not become a confirmed uptrend by that close alone. It is a breakout attempt. Follow-through, a return into the range, or a retest may help clarify the reading. When the visible structure is mixed, write “unclear for now” and keep the trade conditional rather than forcing a label.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Classify the market</h4>\n<p>For each panel, choose <strong>trend</strong>, <strong>range</strong>, or <strong>unclear</strong>. Name two visible facts that support your answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> A series of higher highs and higher lows supports an uptrend. Repeated turns within relatively stable boundaries support a range. Overlapping swings, a recent boundary break without follow-through, or conflicting structure can justify “unclear.” The answer depends on what the figure actually shows, not on guessing the later outcome.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>A market has been moving sideways for several bars, but then one large candle closes above the range high. The bands begin widening. Is the market definitely in an uptrend?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> Not yet. This is a breakout attempt. It may develop into an uptrend if price holds and makes further progress, or it may fail and return to the range. I would keep the context provisional until follow-through clarifies it.</p>","quiz":{"question":"Price has overlapping swings, repeatedly crosses a flat middle band and has no clear progress. What context label may be appropriate?","choices":["Confirmed uptrend.","Confirmed downtrend.","Range or unclear, depending on the boundary evidence."],"correct":2,"why":"Do not force a trend label when the structure does not support it. Describe the overlap and keep the context provisional."}},"3.2":{"title":"When Price Walks Along a Band","timeLabel":"15 minutes","take":"A band walk can be a sign of trend strength. Do not trade against it simply because price is near an outer band.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 3.2</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>Why might selling every upper-band touch in a rising market lead to repeated losses?</p>\n<p>In a strong trend, price can travel along the upper band for several bars. Bollinger calls this <strong>walking the bands</strong>. In an uptrend, price may repeatedly touch or close near the upper band while continuing to make progress. In a downtrend, it may repeatedly interact with the lower band.</p>\n<p>The phrase sounds unusual until you see the pattern. A band touch does not always mean price has travelled too far. Sometimes it shows that price is moving strongly in one direction.</p>\n<h4>Look at the whole sequence</h4>\n<p>One upper-band touch is just one event. A band walk is a sequence. Look for repeated upper-band interaction while the swing highs and lows keep rising. The middle band may also slope upward. Together, these observations support the idea that the market is trending rather than repeatedly returning to the centre.</p>\n<p>The opposite applies during a downtrend. Repeated lower-band interaction, falling swing highs and lows, and a declining middle band may fit continuing downside pressure. Buying every lower-band touch would repeatedly stand against that evidence.</p>\n<p>These features are clues, not a perfect trend detector. Price can briefly move along a band and then turn. The key is to avoid declaring a reversal simply because price is at an outer band.</p>\n<h4>A useful comparison</h4>\n<p>Figure 3.2 places an isolated upper-band touch next to a more persistent sequence. In the isolated case, one candle reaches the band but the rest of the chart may not support a trend conclusion. In the band-walk case, repeated interactions occur alongside directional progress.</p>\n<p>Ask: <strong>What has price been doing between the touches?</strong> If it keeps advancing, the evidence differs from a market that touches the band and returns to the middle repeatedly.</p>\n<h4>What about “overbought” or “oversold”?</h4>\n<p>These terms can be misleading when used as automatic trade instructions. Price near the upper band is relatively high within the current bands. It does not necessarily mean the market is too high to rise further. Price near the lower band is relatively low; it does not necessarily mean the market is too low to fall further.</p>\n<p>If you want to consider a reversal, look for evidence that the prior trend is weakening or that price structure is changing. Do not use the band touch alone as the trigger.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Fading a band walk because the market looks extended.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>A band walk can be a sign of trend strength. Do not trade against it simply because price is near an outer band.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger&#39;s discussion of walking the bands and Official Rule 7 in <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">the official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Worked comparison: Count the progress between touches</h4>\n<p><strong>Case A — Band walk.</strong> Price closes near the upper band on four occasions, but each touch is accompanied by new highs and pullbacks that remain relatively shallow. The middle band slopes upward. The sequence shows continuing progress, so an automatic short at the fourth touch would be a bet against the existing structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Case B — Isolated contact.</strong> Price reaches the upper band once, then falls back toward the middle band and continues to overlap earlier candles. There is no comparable sequence of higher highs and higher lows. The two charts both contain an upper-band touch, but only the first shows evidence of a sustained directional move. Even then, the band walk does not promise another leg. If considering a reversal, identify the actual change in structure and a defined trigger; do not substitute the word “overbought” for that evidence.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Identify the band walk</h4>\n<p>Compare the isolated touch with the band walk in Figure 3.2. Which panel gives stronger evidence of a persistent directional move? Point to the visible features that support your answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The band walk should show repeated interaction with one band while price keeps progressing in the same direction. The isolated touch does not establish that sequence. Neither panel guarantees the next move.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>Price has closed near the lower band for four bars while swing highs and lows continue falling. A learner says, “It is stretched, so I will buy now.”</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> The chart shows persistent downside movement, not a confirmed reversal. I would avoid buying solely from the lower-band position. I would wait for a change in structure and a defined long trigger, or stand aside.</p>","quiz":{"question":"Which feature best distinguishes a band walk from one isolated touch?","choices":["Repeated interaction with the same band while price continues making directional progress.","The first candle must be unusually large.","The outer bands must remain the same width."],"correct":0,"why":"A band walk is a sequence. The movement between touches and the swing structure matter more than one candle's location."}},"3.3":{"title":"Two Ways Traders Talk About a “Squeeze”","timeLabel":"16 minutes","take":"Know which squeeze you are reading. Both describe contraction, but they define it in different ways.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 3.3</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>Do all indicators called a “squeeze” measure the same thing?</p>\n<p>No. The term is used for related ideas, but the definitions are not interchangeable. This matters when you read a book, watch a trader, or open a study on a charting platform.</p>\n<h4>Bollinger&#39;s BandWidth approach</h4>\n<p>BandWidth measures the gap between the upper and lower Bollinger Bands relative to the middle band. When BandWidth falls to an unusually low level compared with its own past, the bands are narrow. Bollinger discusses using this condition to identify a <strong>Squeeze</strong>—a period of low volatility.</p>\n<p>You can picture it as price moving in a more contained way than it did during wider-band periods. That condition may be worth watching because market movement changes over time. But the low width does not give a direction, a precise breakout time, or a guarantee that a large move will follow immediately.</p>\n<h4>The TTM Squeeze approach</h4>\n<p>John Carter&#39;s TTM Squeeze uses a different comparison: it looks at the relationship between Bollinger Bands and Keltner Channels. In the common implementation, the Bollinger Bands contracting inside the Keltner Channels marks the squeeze condition. A momentum component is also commonly displayed to help assess direction.</p>\n<p>The visual logic is different. Bollinger&#39;s BandWidth approach asks, “Are the Bollinger Bands unusually narrow compared with this instrument&#39;s history?” The TTM Squeeze asks, “Have the Bollinger Bands contracted inside the Keltner Channels under this indicator&#39;s settings?”</p>\n<p>They both focus on contraction, but one measures Bollinger Band width relative to the middle band, while the other compares two envelopes. Different platforms may use different settings, so check the study definition rather than assuming every implementation is identical.</p>\n<h4>What is common—and what is not</h4>\n<p>Both approaches draw attention to a quieter period. Neither one removes the need to read price action. A squeeze is context. The breakout direction and whether it holds still need to be assessed from the chart and any clearly defined confirmation rules.</p>\n<p>If a trader uses momentum with a squeeze, that is additional information. Do not confuse that information with the squeeze definition itself.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Using “Bollinger Squeeze” and “TTM Squeeze” as if they are always the same indicator.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Know which squeeze you are reading. Both describe contraction, but they define it in different ways.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger&#39;s official Rule 19 and the Squeeze chapter of <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>. John Carter discusses the TTM Squeeze in <em>Mastering the Trade</em>. The <a href=\"https://toslc.thinkorswim.com/center/reference/Tech-Indicators/studies-library/T-U/TTM-Squeeze.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">thinkorswim TTM Squeeze reference</a> describes the Bollinger Band/Keltner Channel relationship. This lesson distinguishes the concepts rather than attributing Carter&#39;s implementation to Bollinger.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Inspect Figure 3.3</h4>\n<p>In Panel A, look at the BandWidth measure relative to its previous values. In Panel B, look at the relative positions of the Bollinger Bands and Keltner Channels. Describe the difference without assuming one method must always be better.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: Check which squeeze you are using</h4>\n<p><strong>Case A — BandWidth.</strong> Bollinger Bands narrow until the BandWidth pane is near the low end of its own recent range. The squeeze description comes from how narrow the Bollinger envelope is relative to its centre and history. Price may be quiet, but no direction is established.</p>\n<p><strong>Case B — TTM Squeeze.</strong> A chart instead marks a squeeze when the Bollinger Bands are inside the Keltner Channels. That is a relationship between two envelopes. A platform may also display a momentum histogram; its direction is additional information, not part of the definition of BandWidth. It is possible for a trader to call both situations “compression” while the exact indicator conditions differ. Before copying a rule from a lesson or trading platform, check the calculation and settings. Keep the squeeze label, momentum reading and price trigger as three distinct pieces of information.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Distinguish the squeeze definitions</h4>\n<p>Complete the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bollinger BandWidth measures ___.</li>\n<li>The common TTM Squeeze checks whether Bollinger Bands are ___.</li>\n<li>Neither squeeze definition alone tells you ___.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> BandWidth measures relative band width; the TTM Squeeze checks whether Bollinger Bands are inside Keltner Channels; neither alone tells you the direction of the next move.</p>\n<p><strong>Test the reading:</strong> The bands are narrow, but price is still inside its range. What would you need to see before choosing a long or short scenario?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> I would need a defined price boundary and a price event that meets a stated trigger. I would also decide what would invalidate the idea. The squeeze definition alone provides neither direction nor an entry.</p>","quiz":{"question":"What does the common TTM Squeeze definition compare?","choices":["Only the current BandWidth value with a fixed universal number.","The Bollinger Bands with the Keltner Channels; momentum is an additional component.","The closing price with the 20-period average only."],"correct":1,"why":"BandWidth and TTM Squeeze are related but distinct. One measures Bollinger envelope width; the other compares two envelopes."}},"3.4":{"title":"A Squeeze Does Not Choose the Direction","timeLabel":"15 minutes","take":"A squeeze tells you to pay attention to changing volatility. Price must still show which scenario, if any, is developing.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 3.4</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>What should you conclude when the bands are very narrow but price has not broken a meaningful level?</p>\n<p>You have evidence of a quiet period. You do not yet have evidence of a direction. A squeeze can be followed by a sharp upward move, a sharp downward move, a small move that goes nowhere, or an initial break that fails and returns inside the prior range.</p>\n<p>The natural temptation is to anticipate the breakout. But if your evidence is only that the bands are narrow, you are guessing the direction before price has shown it.</p>\n<h4>Prepare scenarios instead of making a prediction</h4>\n<p>Before the breakout, identify a nearby price boundary that matters. This could be the high or low of the compression range, or another clear structural level. Then prepare two scenarios.</p>\n<p><strong>Upward scenario:</strong> If price closes above the chosen boundary and meets the confirmation rule in your plan, you will review the long idea. Specify what would invalidate the idea—for example, a return into the range if your method requires the break to hold.</p>\n<p><strong>Downward scenario:</strong> If price closes below the chosen boundary and meets the mirrored conditions, you will review the short idea. Specify what would invalidate that case.</p>\n<p>You do not need to place both trades. You are preparing for either outcome so you do not have to invent rules when price starts moving.</p>\n<h4>Why the first move can mislead</h4>\n<p>Price can cross a boundary and then quickly return. The first move may be a failed breakout, sometimes called a <strong>head-fake</strong> in Bollinger&#39;s Squeeze discussion. The word describes what happened; it is not a promise that traders can always predict the trap in advance.</p>\n<p>The disciplined response is to define what your breakout plan requires and what cancels it. If price fails the requirement, the original idea is no longer valid. A new trade in the opposite direction needs its own trigger and risk plan; it is not automatic.</p>\n<h4>Use Figure 3.4</h4>\n<p>The three panels begin with similar quiet conditions, then follow different paths. Compare what happens after the initial boundary is crossed. The visual is designed to make one important point clear: the narrow bands alone do not distinguish the outcomes.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Entering just because the bands are narrow, or because price has made its first move without checking whether it meets the plan.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>A squeeze tells you to pay attention to changing volatility. Price must still show which scenario, if any, is developing.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger&#39;s Official Rules 8 and 19 and <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>, chapter on the Squeeze. Specific boundary, confirmation and invalidation rules in this lesson are examples used in this course, not claims that Bollinger prescribed one universal entry system. <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Worked comparison: Prepare both sides before the break</h4>\n<p>Assume price has spent time between 100 and 104 while BandWidth contracts. Before price leaves the range, write two conditional plans. For an upside scenario, identify what completed close would qualify above 104 and whether your method requires a further hold. For a downside scenario, define the mirrored condition below 100. State in advance what would count as failure—for example, a quick return inside the range if holding outside is part of your rule.</p>\n<p>Now compare three outcomes: a close at 105 followed by continued closes above 104; a close at 99 followed by further weakness; and a close at 105 that is immediately followed by a return to 102. They start from similar compression, but the outcomes differ. The correct response is not to predict which one must happen. It is to follow the scenario that meets the written conditions and stand aside if neither develops clearly.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Compare the outcomes</h4>\n<p>For the three outcomes, write:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What would support a long scenario?</li>\n<li>What would support a short scenario?</li>\n<li>What evidence would make you stop treating the first break as valid?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> A long requires the chosen upward trigger and any pre-planned confirmation; a short requires the mirrored downside trigger. A quick return into the range or a failure to meet the stated hold condition can weaken the first-break case. The exact rule depends on the method being tested.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>BandWidth has reached a low reading, but price is in the middle of a broad range with no clear nearby boundary. What is a reasonable decision?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> Wait. Low BandWidth draws attention to a quiet period, but without a clear boundary there is no well-defined breakout trigger. I can keep monitoring without making a directional prediction.</p>","quiz":{"question":"Bands are narrow but there is no clear nearby price boundary. What is a defensible decision?","choices":["Enter long because contraction precedes upward moves.","Enter short because the first breakout is usually false.","Wait until price produces a meaningful, pre-defined trigger."],"correct":2,"why":"Compression may warrant attention, but it does not select a direction or define an entry."}},"4.1":{"title":"Reading a W-Bottom","timeLabel":"17 minutes","take":"The W shape starts the question. The relationship to the bands and the later price evidence help you judge whether a reversal plan is justified.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 4.1</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>When two lows look similar, what could make the second low more informative than the first?</p>\n<p>A W-bottom is a two-low pattern that can help organise a possible change from a decline to a recovery. In Bollinger&#39;s approach, the bands help you judge the lows in relation to recent price variation. A common example has the first low reach or move below the lower band, while the second low has a different relationship to the band. In some W-type patterns, the second price low can be lower than the first while the band relationship shows less relative weakness.</p>\n<p>The idea is not simply “two lows make a W, therefore buy.” The shape and the band&#39;s relationship to each low are part of the evidence. Price still needs to show that the downside attempt has failed or that the structure is turning.</p>\n<h4>Work through the structure</h4>\n<p>Look at Figure 4.1 and identify:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The first low.</li>\n<li>The move away from that low.</li>\n<li>The second low.</li>\n<li>The intervening swing high that price would need to overcome under the course&#39;s example confirmation rule.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The first low may appear at or below the lower band, showing a relatively low position. The second low should be judged in relation to the band and to the first low, rather than by looking at price alone. If price makes a lower low but the second low is no longer outside the lower band, the relative position may be improving even though price has dipped further. That contrast is what makes the pattern worth investigating.</p>\n<p>It is still only a candidate. Price can remain weak, make another low, or move sideways without confirming a recovery. It is also possible for a W shape to form in the middle of a broader downtrend without leading to a lasting reversal.</p>\n<h4>What confirmation means here</h4>\n<p>For practice, we will use a clear confirmation rule: price moves above the intervening swing high between the two lows. This gives the setup a condition that can be identified and tested consistently. It is an example rule for this course, not a universal rule that Bollinger prescribed in this exact form.</p>\n<p>The trigger does not guarantee success. It simply makes the decision specific: the learner can identify what event must occur, where the idea is invalidated, and how much risk the plan permits.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Buying every pair of lows that looks like the letter W.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The W shape starts the question. The relationship to the bands and the later price evidence help you judge whether a reversal plan is justified.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger discusses W-type bottoms in <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>, Chapter 12, and refers to the use of the bands to clarify common patterns. See the <a href=\"https://www.mheducation.com/highered/mhp/product/bollinger-bollinger-bands\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">publisher&#39;s contents</a>. The intervening-swing trigger in this lesson is a teaching framework, not presented as a verbatim author rule.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Compare the candidate and the lookalike</h4>\n<p>In the second panel, the two lows look similar but the important relationship or confirming move is missing. The correct answer may be “candidate, but not confirmed” or “unclear,” rather than forcing the W-bottom label.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: The relationship between the two lows matters</h4>\n<p>Suppose the first decline reaches 94 while the lower band is around 96, then price recovers toward 100. Later, price declines again to 96 but remains inside the bands. The second low is higher in relative band terms, which is consistent with the W-bottom concept: the second push has not reproduced the first low&#39;s relationship to the lower band. The structure is still a candidate, not a completed trade.</p>\n<p>For a contrasting case, the second decline closes below the lower band again and breaks beneath the first low. That does not support the same reading; downside pressure has not shown the expected weakening. In either case, specify the confirmation you are using—such as a break of the intervening swing high in your course-defined practice rule—and the point that invalidates the idea. Do not buy merely because two lows resemble the letter W.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Compare the two lows</h4>\n<p>In Figure 4.1, mark the two lows, describe how each sits relative to the lower band, and identify what event would confirm the course&#39;s example long plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The first low provides the initial reference. The second low&#39;s relationship to the lower band may show a change in relative weakness. A break above the intervening swing high is the chosen course trigger. If it never occurs, the plan remains inactive.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>A chart forms two lows, but the second low closes below the lower band and price then continues making lower lows. What is the cautious reading?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> The shape resembles a double bottom, but the expected change has not appeared. The continuing lows support ongoing weakness. I would not treat the shape alone as a buy signal.</p>","quiz":{"question":"The first low is below the lower band and the second low stays inside it. What does this suggest?","choices":["A W-bottom candidate worth evaluating, with confirmation still required.","A guaranteed bullish reversal.","A sell signal because two lows have formed."],"correct":0,"why":"The relative relationship between the lows and bands is informative, but the pattern is not complete merely because it resembles a W."}},"4.2":{"title":"Reading an M-Top","timeLabel":"17 minutes","take":"A possible M-top warns you to examine the second high carefully. It does not give you permission to short without a plan.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 4.2</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>What changes if price makes a second high but the relationship to the upper band is weaker than it was at the first high?</p>\n<p>An M-top is a two-high pattern that can help you examine whether an upward move is losing strength. In Bollinger&#39;s approach, the bands help assess the highs in relation to recent price behaviour. A common candidate has the first high reach or move beyond the upper band, while the second high remains inside it. The price highs may be similar, or the second price high may be slightly higher while its relative position is weaker.</p>\n<p>This is a clue to investigate, not proof of a top. A market can form two highs and then break upward. The pattern needs context and a clear condition for confirmation.</p>\n<h4>Read the two highs in sequence</h4>\n<p>Start with the first high. Price reaching or passing the upper band gives a relative high reading. Then watch the reaction away from that high. Price moves down and forms an intervening low. Later, it returns toward the prior high area.</p>\n<p>Now compare the second high with the bands. If the second high cannot reach the same relative position, that difference may suggest the upward movement is not behaving as strongly as before. It may be a warning worth examining, especially if other evidence also suggests the trend is weakening.</p>\n<p>But several alternatives remain possible. The market could be consolidating before another push upward. It could form a wider range. The apparent second high may be too incomplete to interpret. The M shape alone does not tell you which explanation will win.</p>\n<h4>A confirmation rule for practice</h4>\n<p>For practice, this course uses a break below the intervening swing low as one possible confirmation trigger for a short scenario. This makes the rule observable and testable. It is a framework used in this course, not a claim that every M-top must be traded this way.</p>\n<p>Before a trade, define the point that would show the idea is wrong, such as price reclaiming the high area under your plan. Set the stop around the invalidating structure rather than placing it at an arbitrary band line. Then check whether the available distance to a reasonable target justifies the risk.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Shorting the second high before price has shown a meaningful change.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>A possible M-top warns you to examine the second high carefully. It does not give you permission to short without a plan.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger discusses M-type tops in <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>, Chapter 13. The course&#39;s intervening-low trigger is a testable teaching implementation, not an author-attributed universal rule.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Compare it with a continuing trend</h4>\n<p>Figure 4.2 shows two similar highs in an uptrend. If price continues making higher lows, holds above the middle band much of the time, and breaks upward again, the bearish interpretation weakens. The second high&#39;s relationship to the band may prompt attention, but price behaviour must still be respected.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: A weaker second push is only one clue</h4>\n<p>Suppose the first rally reaches 110 while the upper band is near 108. Price then pulls back toward 104 and rallies again to 109, this time staying closer to or inside the upper band. The second push has a different relationship to the bands, which is worth noting. It does not by itself prove that buyers have lost control or that a reversal has begun.</p>\n<p>Compare a chart where the second push also makes a new high, closes firmly beyond the upper band and is followed by further upward progress. A shape that looks like an M can still be part of continuation. For practice, choose a clear structural confirmation and invalidation—such as a break of the swing low between the highs—and apply it consistently. That is a course execution rule, not a claim that every M-top has one universally required entry trigger.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Compare the two highs</h4>\n<p>Mark the two highs and the intervening swing low in Figure 4.2. Explain what makes the first panel a possible M-top and what remains missing from the second.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> A different relationship between the two highs and the upper band can support a candidate M-top. The chosen confirmation event and invalidation condition decide whether the candidate becomes a testable plan. A similar-looking pair of highs without those conditions is not enough.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>The second high is inside the upper band, but price then breaks above both highs and continues rising. What should you conclude?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> The possible M-top did not lead to the expected downside move. The breakout above the highs contradicts the short thesis. The pattern should not be defended simply because it was visible earlier.</p>","quiz":{"question":"A possible M-top has a weaker second push relative to the upper band. What is still required?","choices":["An immediate short at the second high.","A defined confirmation and invalidation, plus a separate risk assessment.","Proof that every M-shaped chart reverses."],"correct":1,"why":"The mismatch is a clue rather than a complete entry instruction. Price can still continue upward."}},"4.3":{"title":"When Price and an Indicator Tell Different Stories","timeLabel":"15 minutes","take":"A mismatch is a reason to investigate. Confirmation and invalidation still need to come from a defined plan.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 4.3</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>If price makes a new high but an indicator does not, should you immediately sell?</p>\n<p>No. A difference between price and an indicator can be useful, but it is not a stand-alone entry signal. It tells you that two views of the recent movement are not matching in the same way. You still need to judge what the difference means and what evidence would confirm a change.</p>\n<h4>What is a divergence-like mismatch?</h4>\n<p>Imagine price makes a new swing high. At the same time, %B does not reach the same high reading as it did at the prior swing. The price has reached a new high, but its relative position within the bands is weaker. That mismatch may suggest the move is losing strength.</p>\n<p>Notice the careful wording: <em>may suggest</em>. There are other explanations. The bands themselves may have widened, changing the relative position. Price may be pausing before another advance. The indicator may be noisy at the chosen timeframe. The trend may continue despite the mismatch.</p>\n<p>The chart does not show hidden certainty. It shows evidence that may change how closely you watch the next move.</p>\n<h4>Use indicators that add different information</h4>\n<p>Bollinger&#39;s rules encourage combining bands with suitable indicators, but warn against simply adding multiple versions of the same thing. Two momentum indicators may largely repeat each other&#39;s message. A momentum measure and a volume-based measure can sometimes contribute different information.</p>\n<p>You do not need a crowded chart. One additional measure is enough if you can explain what question it answers. For example, %B helps describe relative band position. A separately chosen momentum indicator might help describe whether momentum is changing. Volume can help describe recorded trading activity in markets where the volume data is meaningful. None can prove a future outcome.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Treating divergence as a guaranteed reversal signal.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>A mismatch is a reason to investigate. Confirmation and invalidation still need to come from a defined plan.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger&#39;s Official Rules 3–5 and 16 discuss combining Bollinger Bands with other indicators and using %B for pattern recognition. <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Read Figure 4.3</h4>\n<p>In the first panel, identify the new price high and compare the %B reading at the two highs. In the alternative panel, notice that price continues upward after a similar mismatch. The purpose is to show why divergence-like evidence deserves attention but should not be treated as a timer.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: A mismatch raises a question, not a verdict</h4>\n<p>Imagine price falls to a new low while %B does not reach as low a reading as it did on the previous decline. The two measurements do not move in the same way. This may be a clue that the new price low is less extreme relative to the current bands, but the bands themselves have changed, so the relationship needs careful description. It is not direct proof that selling pressure has ended.</p>\n<p>On a second chart, price and %B make new lows together and the decline keeps extending. That sequence offers less support for a reversal interpretation. Neither comparison tells you what happens next. Record the exact mismatch, identify another plausible explanation, and wait for the confirmation condition selected for the setup. A divergence-like relationship is useful because it prompts a closer inspection—not because it makes the next candle predictable.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Describe the mismatch</h4>\n<p>Write two possible explanations for the mismatch in Figure 4.3. Then name one future price event that would support a short scenario and one that would weaken it.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> Possible explanations include weakening relative movement or a temporary pause within a continuing trend. A break below a meaningful swing low may support the downside case under a defined plan; a fresh high with continued upward structure may weaken it. The precise trigger depends on the chosen method.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>You add three momentum indicators to confirm a possible M-top. All three turn down on the same bars. Have you necessarily added three independent pieces of evidence?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> No. If the indicators are closely related, they may repeat much of the same information. More indicators do not automatically create stronger confirmation. I should know what distinct question each tool answers and avoid treating a cluster of similar measures as independent proof.</p>","quiz":{"question":"Price makes a new low while %B does not reach its prior low. What is the most careful interpretation?","choices":["Selling has definitely ended.","The next candle must reverse.","The readings disagree; describe the mismatch, consider alternatives and wait for the setup's confirmation."],"correct":2,"why":"A divergence-like relationship prompts investigation. It does not establish the cause or guarantee a turn."}},"4.4":{"title":"Is the Pattern Complete Enough to Trade?","timeLabel":"14 minutes","take":"A pattern is one part of a plan. It does not replace context, a trigger, invalidation, or risk assessment.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 4.4</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>At what point does a shape on a chart become a trade plan?</p>\n<p>A pattern is a way to organise what you see. It is not complete merely because the past price path resembles a letter. Before a pattern can support a trade, you need to know the context, the specific event that would trigger the trade, where the idea would be wrong, and whether the potential reward justifies the risk.</p>\n<h4>Four different states</h4>\n<p>Figure 4.4 shows four states that can look similar at first glance.</p>\n<p><strong>Candidate:</strong> Some of the expected shape is present, but the key confirmation has not appeared. It is worth watching, not yet a trade.</p>\n<p><strong>Confirmed under the lesson&#39;s rule:</strong> The required price event has occurred. “Confirmed” means the stated condition has been met; it does not mean the trade is guaranteed to win.</p>\n<p><strong>Failed:</strong> Price has moved in a way that contradicts the original idea. The correct response is to cancel or review the plan according to its rules, not reinterpret every new candle to keep the idea alive.</p>\n<p><strong>Unclear:</strong> The structure is too messy, a necessary swing is missing, or the evidence conflicts. You do not need to label every chart.</p>\n<h4>Keep the decision point visible</h4>\n<p>One of the hardest traps is hindsight. After price has moved, a neat pattern may seem obvious. But traders have to decide with the information available at the time. When you inspect a historical example, mentally stop the chart at the original decision point. Ask what was visible then and what remained unknown.</p>\n<p>A course confirmation rule can help make the exercise testable. For example, this course may use a break of the intervening swing high after a W-bottom candidate or a break of the intervening swing low after an M-top candidate. Those are not guarantees and should not be described as universal rules from Bollinger unless the source verification supports that precise attribution.</p>\n<h4>The plan still needs risk logic</h4>\n<p>Even a pattern that has met its chosen trigger can be a poor trade. The stop may need to be too far away, the next obstacle may be too close, or a major event may create risks the chart does not show. The correct decision may still be no trade.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Assuming that once a pattern is identified, an entry must follow.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>A pattern is one part of a plan. It does not replace context, a trigger, invalidation, or risk assessment.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> Pattern categories and the W/M treatments are grounded in John Bollinger&#39;s <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>, Chapters 10–13. The state labels and confirmation rules are teaching frameworks used in this course.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Worked comparison: Four pattern decisions that look similar at first</h4>\n<p><strong>Candidate:</strong> The two lows or highs are visible, but the relative band relationship or confirming price event is missing. Continue observing; do not label it a completed trade.</p>\n<p><strong>Meets the stated rule:</strong> The required relationship and the chosen confirmation are present. The pattern can now be evaluated as a setup, but it still needs a stop, room to the next obstacle and a risk amount that fits the account.</p>\n<p><strong>Failed:</strong> Price crosses the level that invalidates the pattern, or continues in a way that contradicts the premise. Apply the planned invalidation rather than rewriting the pattern after the fact.</p>\n<p><strong>Unclear:</strong> Swings overlap or the evidence can reasonably support more than one interpretation. Keep the case unresolved. A pattern does not become tradable just because a learner can name it; completion, context and execution conditions all matter.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Classify the pattern</h4>\n<p>Classify each panel in Figure 4.4 as candidate, confirmed under the stated course rule, failed, or unclear. For each answer, point to one visible reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The label must follow the rule given with the figure. A candidate lacks the trigger, a confirmed example has met it, a failed case contradicts the plan, and an unclear case lacks enough reliable structure to decide. If two readings remain reasonable, explain the ambiguity rather than guessing.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>A W-bottom candidate has met the course trigger, but the entry is far above the invalidation point and the next resistance area is close. What should you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> The pattern trigger has occurred, but the trade may have poor reward relative to risk. I can skip it. A valid pattern is not a requirement to trade.</p>","quiz":{"question":"A pattern meets its confirmation rule, but the next resistance is too close for the stop distance. What should you do?","choices":["Record a valid pattern but pass on the trade because the risk-to-room trade-off is poor.","Move the stop closer until the ratio looks attractive.","Enter because confirmation overrides risk."],"correct":0,"why":"A confirmed pattern and a suitable trade are different judgements. The stop must fit the structure, not the desired ratio."}},"5.1":{"title":"Bollinger Method I: Trade the Volatility Breakout","timeLabel":"25–28 minutes","take":"Method I buys an upside breakout or shorts a downside breakout after unusually quiet conditions. It does not try to guess direction during the squeeze.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 5.1</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>Why would a trader buy when price breaks above the upper band instead of selling because price looks high?</p>\n<p>Because Bollinger&#39;s first method is built around a different idea: very quiet movement may be followed by a period of wider movement. If you wait for the breakout after a squeeze, you are trying to join the expansion—not predict a reversal just because price reaches an outer band.</p>\n<h4>First, identify the squeeze</h4>\n<p>BandWidth measures the gap between the upper and lower Bollinger Bands relative to the middle band. A squeeze is not simply “the bands look a bit narrow.” Bollinger looks for BandWidth to be unusually low compared with its own history. In the book, he describes using a longer lookback—about six months as a default in that discussion—so the squeeze means unusually quiet movement for that instrument rather than a fixed width that must fit every market.</p>\n<p>His later official TradeStation Method I package uses a 125-period low in BandWidth as its default squeeze definition. It also allows a recent squeeze to qualify for an alert even if the bands have already started widening. Those details belong to the software implementation; don&#39;t mix the package&#39;s exact alert settings with the broader book explanation.</p>\n<h4>The long setup</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Observe:</strong> BandWidth has reached a very low level relative to its history. Price has been contained, and the bands are close together.</li>\n<li><strong>Interpret:</strong> The market may be ready to move into a wider-volatility phase. The squeeze does not tell you whether the move will be up or down.</li>\n<li><strong>Test:</strong> Wait for price to break above the upper Bollinger Band. Under Method I, that is the long-side volatility-breakout event. Do not enter simply because the bands are narrow.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>The short setup</h4>\n<p>Use the same logic in the opposite direction. Identify the squeeze first, then wait for price to break below the lower band. That downside break is the short-side volatility-breakout event.</p>\n<p>Notice the important difference from a mean-reversion trade: Method I does <strong>not</strong> sell the upper band or buy the lower band. It looks to trade the move beyond the band after a squeeze.</p>\n<h4>From squeeze to executable trade: prepare, trigger, enter, protect</h4>\n<p>The question is fair: <strong>a close-confirmed breakout will usually give you a worse price than buying before the close.</strong> That is the trade-off. Waiting makes the rule easier to reproduce and filters some temporary spikes, but it cannot guarantee that the move has enough room left. Entering early improves the price only if the breakout continues; it also exposes you to more false starts. There is no entry that gives both the earliest price and confirmation.</p>\n<p><strong>Before the breakout, prepare these things:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Find a genuinely low BandWidth reading relative to that instrument’s own recent history. Mark the current range high and low, the nearby swing levels, and obvious resistance/support beyond the range.</li>\n<li>Decide what counts as the breakout before it happens. For this lesson, the primary, repeatable trigger is a completed bar closing beyond the relevant band after the squeeze condition. A wick through the band that closes back inside is not the same event under this classroom rule.</li>\n<li>Set alerts around the band/range edge and decide the maximum loss you will accept. The squeeze is the time to plan—not the time to guess long or short.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Choose and record one entry style before reviewing results:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Close-confirmation entry — the default for this course.</strong> After a squeeze, act only after a completed close beyond the upper band for a long or lower band for a short. The trade is not “too late” by definition, but it can be too extended in a particular chart. Check the stop distance and the next obstacle before accepting the entry.</li>\n<li><strong>Earlier breakout entry — an aggressive course variant.</strong> Enter as price crosses a pre-marked breakout level while the bar is still forming. This can get a better price, but the bar can return inside the band before closing. You must define which level is monitored and test the rule without looking ahead; do not call it the same thing as a confirmed-close entry.</li>\n<li><strong>Retest entry — a separate course variant.</strong> Let price break out first, then wait for a pullback/retest that holds near the broken range edge or band, and enter only when price resumes through the pullback’s short-term swing. It may reduce chasing, but some breakouts never retest. Do not treat a retest as a requirement of Bollinger’s basic Method I.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Place the stop where the trade idea is wrong—not at a convenient arbitrary distance.</strong></p>\n<p>For a long, a tight execution stop might sit below the breakout bar’s low; a wider structural stop might sit below the consolidation/range low or the retest swing low. For a short, use the corresponding high. The wider level is less likely to be hit by ordinary noise but increases the cash risk per unit, so position size must fall. Bollinger discusses a modified Parabolic stop or the opposite-band tag as ways to manage an open Method I position; those are management/exit choices, not a universal initial stop that suits every entry.</p>\n<p>Calculate quantity from the cash risk you permit divided by the entry-to-stop distance, allowing for costs and slippage. If the valid structural stop is so far away that the position must be tiny, or the next resistance/support gives too little room, <strong>skip it</strong>. The rule can trigger while the trade is still poor.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure clues:</strong> a breakout that quickly closes back in the old range, a failed push followed by a strong move the other way, or no follow-through after the first expansion. These are warnings to follow your prewritten stop/exit rule; they do not automatically authorise a reverse trade.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Method I buys an upside breakout or shorts a downside breakout after unusually quiet conditions. It does not try to guess direction during the squeeze.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger, <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>, Chapter 16, “Method I: Volatility Breakout”; <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/_files/ugd/58be43_f5e967053af44fa083340cacfc5e6226.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">official Method I manual</a>. Bollinger&#39;s book describes the method and its head-fake risk; the official software manual documents one specific 125-period/alert-lookback implementation.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Historical chart examples to compare</h4>\n<p>Treat these as case studies, not a list of guaranteed winners. On each chart, mark where compression ends, the exact qualifying bar, the price you could realistically enter, the structural stop, and the first nearby obstacle. At least one example is included because a first break can fail before price resolves.</p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.1.1 — Narrow bands and a subsequent upside expansion.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.1.2 — A historical volatility squeeze followed by a price breakout.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.1.3 — Compression and breakout behaviour on a different chart.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.1.4 — A downside head fake followed by a later resolution.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.1.5 — A breakout-versus-head-fake comparison.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Practice prompt:</strong> On each chart, compare a close-confirmation entry with an earlier entry. Which gets a better price? Which would be stopped if the initial move failed? If you cannot point to a stop before entry, the chart is not yet a complete trade plan.</p>\n<h4>Read the examples</h4>\n<p>In Figure 5.1, compare the upward breakout, downward breakout, and failed first move. The squeeze provides the shared starting condition; the direction only becomes visible when price moves.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> For each panel, identify the squeeze, the first breakout direction, and whether Method I would have a long alert, a short alert, or an unresolved/failed first attempt under the illustrated rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> A break above the upper band following a squeeze fits the long case; a break below the lower band fits the short case. The failed first move shows why you should plan for failure rather than assume the first move must continue. Whether a particular entry is acceptable still depends on the stop, available reward, and your execution rules.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: Breakout condition versus a false start</h4>\n<p>Assume BandWidth has contracted while price remains between 100 and 104. A completed candle then closes at 105, beyond the upper band and range high. Under the course baseline, this is the point to assess the long scenario—not the earlier moment when the bands became narrow. Before entry, check where the setup would be invalidated and whether the distance to the next resistance area leaves adequate room.</p>\n<p>A contrasting chart also closes at 105 but the next candle falls back to 102 and closes inside the old range. The first close looked like a breakout, yet follow-through did not appear. A rule that requires a later hold would reject this case; a basic breakout plan still needs its stated stop or failure rule. The return inside is not automatically a short. Reassess and wait for a fresh, separately defined setup.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Try a new case</h4>\n<p>BandWidth is at a six-month low, but price remains inside the recent range. Should you buy now?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> Not under the basic breakout rule. The squeeze tells me to watch for an expansion; it does not give me an entry or a direction. I would wait for the chosen breakout event.</p>","quiz":{"question":"Under the course baseline for Method I, what activates the breakout assessment?","choices":["The bands first become narrow.","After a squeeze, a completed close beyond the relevant band under the selected rule.","Any touch of an outer band."],"correct":1,"why":"The squeeze prepares the trader to watch. The defined breakout event—not the compression alone—triggers the next assessment."}},"5.2":{"title":"Bollinger Method II: Follow Strength Confirmed by MFI","timeLabel":"25–28 minutes","take":"Method II buys strength when %B and MFI both confirm it, and sells weakness when both are weak.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 5.2</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>How can you use the bands to join a move instead of trying to call a top or bottom?</p>\n<p>Bollinger&#39;s <strong>Method II — Trend Following</strong> combines price strength with strength in a volume-based indicator. The indicator is the Money Flow Index (MFI). Unlike Method I, this method does not require a squeeze first. It looks for price and the indicator to confirm one another.</p>\n<h4>The two pieces of evidence</h4>\n<p><strong>%B</strong> describes where the latest close sits relative to the Bollinger Bands. A value of 0 is the lower band, 0.5 is the middle, and 1 is the upper band. A value above 0.8 places price in the upper part of the band range.</p>\n<p><strong>MFI</strong> combines price and volume information to estimate buying and selling pressure. It ranges from 0 to 100. Bollinger&#39;s Method II example uses MFI with a 10-period length and treats readings above 80 as strong and below 20 as weak.</p>\n<p>The point is to require both pieces to agree. A strong price position without strong MFI does not meet the published example rule; nor does a strong MFI reading without the required price position.</p>\n<h4>The long setup</h4>\n<p>Use Bollinger&#39;s stated example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>%B is greater than 0.8.</li>\n<li>MFI(10) is greater than 80.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When both conditions are true, Method II gives a buy signal. The idea is to join a strong move when price strength is confirmed by money-flow strength.</p>\n<p>Do not reinterpret this as a mean-reversion entry because price is near the upper band. It is a trend-following setup. The conditions are intentionally looking for strength, not a reason to sell.</p>\n<h4>The short setup</h4>\n<p>The published counterpart is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>%B is less than 0.2.</li>\n<li>MFI(10) is less than 20.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When both conditions are true, Method II gives a sell/short signal. Price is in the lower part of the bands and MFI confirms weakness.</p>\n<h4>From a Method II signal to a trade</h4>\n<p><strong>Early clues:</strong> watch for price pushing into the upper part of the bands while MFI(10) strengthens, or for price pressing into the lower part while MFI weakens. These are watch conditions, not separate signals. The stated Method II example does not require a squeeze.</p>\n<p><strong>The trigger:</strong> for the long, a completed bar must have %B above 0.8 and MFI(10) above 80. For the short, %B must be below 0.2 and MFI(10) below 20. Use the same bar-close convention consistently so you do not count a threshold that was briefly crossed mid-bar and then disappeared.</p>\n<p><strong>Entry choices:</strong> the simpler course rule is to act at the close of the qualifying bar, if the remaining reward justifies the stop. A more cautious course variant is to wait for price to break the signal bar’s high for a long or low for a short; this avoids some immediate failures but gives up price and can miss the trade. If price has already travelled far from the recent structure, do not chase solely because both readings remain extreme.</p>\n<p><strong>Stop and size:</strong> Bollinger discusses a modified Parabolic stop or an opposite-band tag as exit approaches; the related published strategy notes also mention Parabolic SAR. For a fixed-risk practice plan, mark the recent swing/pullback that would invalidate the continuation idea, and size the trade from that distance. A stop below the signal candle for a long (above for a short) is a tighter course variant, not a universal author rule. If the stop is wide or a nearby resistance/support leaves poor reward relative to risk, pass.</p>\n<p><strong>What weakens it:</strong> the indicators do not agree, the threshold is only touched intrabar, the move is already extended, or price immediately loses the breakout area. A signal is evidence to consider a trade, not an obligation to enter.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Method II buys strength when %B and MFI both confirm it, and sells weakness when both are weak.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger, <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>, Chapter 19, “Method II: Trend Following”; <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/_files/ugd/58be43_b120ddf0184540608baf19e2c0ae2019.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">official Method II manual</a>. The 0.8/80 buy and 0.2/20 sell thresholds are Bollinger&#39;s published example conditions.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Historical chart examples to compare</h4>\n<p>These examples deliberately include different outcomes. Check the %B and MFI panes wherever they are shown. A price thrust without both thresholds is a near-miss, while a correct trigger can still fail.</p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.2.1 — Price, %B and MFI together during an established uptrend.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.2.2 — A buy and sell sequence with %B and MFI.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.2.3 — A historical long and short signal with a mixed outcome.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.2.4 — A second buy case, including the risk of a failed signal.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.2.5 — A sell setup that follows price and money-flow weakness.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.2.6 — A filtered signal where additional confirmation changes the timing.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Practice prompt:</strong> For every example, record the first bar where both published thresholds are true, then compare entry-at-close with entry on the signal bar’s break. Did the later confirmation improve the trade, make it too late, or stop a losing signal?</p>\n<h4>Read Figure 5.2</h4>\n<p>Panel A shows the long conditions coming together. Panel B shows the mirrored short conditions. Panel C shows a tempting price move where MFI does not confirm; it is included to make the missing condition visible.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> Identify which panels meet Method II&#39;s stated conditions. For each one that does not qualify, state whether %B or MFI is missing its threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> A valid long needs %B above 0.8 and MFI(10) above 80. A valid short needs %B below 0.2 and MFI(10) below 20. If one condition is missing, the documented setup has not fired even if the chart looks attractive.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: Price strength and money-flow strength must agree</h4>\n<p>Suppose the configured Method II rule calls for %B to reach its strength threshold while MFI also meets the method&#39;s confirmation condition. Price has been making higher swing lows and the close continues above the relevant level. Both pieces of indicator evidence are aligned, and the context supports investigating the long setup. This still is not a reason to skip the stop, target and risk calculation.</p>\n<p>Now imagine %B meets the price-position condition but MFI remains weak or fails the required threshold. That is not “almost the same” signal if the rule requires both. Do not loosen the condition after seeing the chart. Check the correct indicator variant, settings and data, then record whether each condition was present on the completed bar. A confirmation rule is useful only if it is applied consistently to the examples that work and those that fail.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Try a new case</h4>\n<p>%B is 0.93, but MFI(10) is 64. Is this a Method II buy?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> No. Price is in the upper part of the bands, but MFI does not meet the stated strength threshold. I can keep watching, but I should not call it a Method II signal.</p>","quiz":{"question":"The %B condition qualifies but MFI does not meet the stated confirmation rule. What should happen?","choices":["Ignore MFI because one indicator is enough.","Enter a smaller trade automatically.","Do not label it a qualifying Method II signal if the method requires both conditions."],"correct":2,"why":"A rule requiring both price position and MFI confirmation must be applied as written, including to examples that do not qualify."}},"5.3":{"title":"Bollinger Method III: Look for Reversal Clues in Volume","timeLabel":"25–28 minutes","take":"Method III looks for a band extreme that the volume evidence does not confirm, then waits for price to support the reversal idea.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 5.3</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>What if price reaches an outer band, but a volume-based indicator does not confirm the move?</p>\n<p>Bollinger&#39;s <strong>Method III — Reversals</strong> is designed for this kind of disagreement. It combines price position with a volume indicator to look for signs that buying or selling pressure may be changing. The idea is not to buy every lower-band touch or sell every upper-band touch. It is to look for a band extreme that conflicts with the indicator evidence.</p>\n<h4>The basic logic</h4>\n<p>A tag of the lower band says price is relatively low. If a volume indicator remains positive, the selling move may not be as convincing as the price alone suggests. This can create a <strong>buy alert</strong>.</p>\n<p>A tag of the upper band says price is relatively high. If a volume indicator is negative, the move may not be as strong as the price alone suggests. This can create a <strong>sell/short alert</strong>.</p>\n<p>The indicator needs to add information. Bollinger&#39;s original book discusses tools such as Intraday Intensity, Accumulation/Distribution, MFI and VWMACD in different examples. These tools are not identical, so do not swap them around while claiming the results are the same method.</p>\n<h4>From reversal alert to executable trade</h4>\n<p>This is a reversal method, so the main danger is acting while the original trend is still strong.</p>\n<p><strong>Early clues:</strong> price is testing an outer band and the chosen volume-based indicator is not confirming the latest push. Decide which documented indicator variant you are using before looking at the result: for example, the software version using Intraday Intensity, or the book example using the stated %B and volume-indicator conditions. Do not interchange indicator names and keep the same threshold as if nothing changed.</p>\n<p><strong>Long entry sequence:</strong> a lower-band tag plus positive Intraday Intensity creates the documented buy alert in one official implementation. Do not buy solely on the tag. Wait for the specified strong up-bar confirmation. For a repeatable execution plan, choose either entry at the confirmation bar’s close or a course variant that triggers only if price breaks above that bar’s high. If the price continues making lower lows and the confirmation bar never appears, there is no entry.</p>\n<p><strong>Short entry sequence:</strong> an upper-band tag plus negative Intraday Intensity creates the corresponding alert. Wait for a strong down bar, then use its close or a break below its low as the preselected trigger. A weak bar that closes high in its range is not the same confirmation.</p>\n<p><strong>Stop and target:</strong> for a long, define invalidation just below the retest/reversal low; for a short, above the reversal high. A stop beyond the extreme gives the idea room, but that means fewer units for the same cash risk. Bollinger discusses the opposite band as one possible target in examples; first check whether that target gives adequate room. A nearby stop with a distant target is not enough if the signal itself has not confirmed.</p>\n<p><strong>Stand aside when:</strong> the volume indicator agrees with the price move instead of contradicting it, price is persistently walking the band with no sign of failure, the volume feed is unreliable for the instrument, or the confirming bar would force an entry too far from the invalidation level. A divergence-like appearance can last longer than expected.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Method III looks for a band extreme that the volume evidence does not confirm, then waits for price to support the reversal idea.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger, <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>, Chapter 20, “Method III: Reversals”; <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/_files/ugd/58be43_377f4254baa04a19aaadb1735b45b6f0.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">official Method III manual</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>A concrete long example from the book</h4>\n<p>One of Bollinger&#39;s systematised examples is to look for %B below 0.05 while the Intraday Intensity measure is above zero. In plain language, price is very near or below the lower band, while the volume-based indicator points in the positive direction.</p>\n<p>The next step is important: Bollinger recommends waiting for price to confirm the idea—for example, a strong up day—before acting. The alert points to a possible reversal; price confirmation is the next condition to test.</p>\n<p>In another version of Method III, Bollinger describes a W-bottom whose second low has a higher %B reading than the first. The student then checks whether a volume oscillator such as MFI or VWMACD shows a similar improvement. If it does, the book&#39;s example is to buy the first strong up day; if it does not, wait.</p>\n<h4>A concrete short example</h4>\n<p>For a short-side reversal, Bollinger gives an example using %B above 0.95 together with a negative Accumulation/Distribution reading. The idea is that price has reached an extreme near the upper band while the independent volume measure is not confirming the strength of that move.</p>\n<p>Bollinger&#39;s later official software Method III manual uses another specific implementation: lower-band tag plus positive Intraday Intensity creates a buy alert; upper-band tag plus negative Intraday Intensity creates a sell/short alert. It also recommends waiting for a strong up bar after the buy alert or a strong down bar after the short alert. Treat these as documented variations, not as if every indicator combination were identical.</p>\n<h4>Historical chart examples to compare</h4>\n<p>These charts show reversal clues and indicator disagreement in different markets. Use them to practise locating the extreme, the indicator evidence, the price-confirmation bar and the invalidation point. Not every chart below contains every variable required by the same Method III implementation.</p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.3.1 — A lower-band reversal case with a positive volume-indicator clue.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.3.2 — An upper-band reversal case with a weakening volume-indicator clue.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.3.3 — Bollinger Bands, Intraday Intensity and volume displayed for comparison.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.3.4 — A reversal-shaped price structure that still needs indicator and price confirmation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.3.5 — A lower-band W-shaped turn for comparison with the reversal rules.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Practice prompt:</strong> For each case, list what the chart actually proves and what is still missing. A pattern shape alone does not prove the volume condition; a volume clue alone does not prove that price has confirmed the reversal.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: Divergence and confirmation are different stages</h4>\n<p><strong>Potential long:</strong> Price makes a lower low at the lower band, but the volume-based indicator fails to make a matching low. That disagreement raises a reversal question. The next step is to wait for the price-confirmation condition defined by the method and decide where a long thesis would be wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Potential short:</strong> Price pushes to a new high near the upper band, while the selected volume indicator is weaker than it was on the prior push. Again, the mismatch is an alert rather than an entry. The move can still continue despite the disagreement. In both directions, label the sequence correctly: band interaction, indicator disagreement, price confirmation, then separate execution and risk decisions. Do not treat a related W-bottom or M-top as proof that the Method III indicator conditions are present; the required evidence must be checked directly.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Task</h4>\n<p>Use Figure 5.3 to identify the band tag, the volume-indicator reading, and the later price-confirmation bar. Which case creates a buy alert, and which creates a sell/short alert under the stated version of Method III?</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> A lower-band tag with positive Intraday Intensity fits the buy-alert example. An upper-band tag with a negative indicator fits the short-alert example. The later price bar matters because the alert is not an instruction to enter immediately.</p>\n<h4>Try a new case</h4>\n<p>Price touches the lower band, but Intraday Intensity is negative and price continues to make lower lows. Does the positive-volume reversal setup qualify?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> No. The required indicator disagreement is missing, and price continues to weaken. I would not turn a band tag into a buy signal.</p>","quiz":{"question":"A price extreme is not matched by the chosen volume-based indicator. What is that disagreement?","choices":["A reversal alert to investigate; price confirmation and risk planning still follow.","Automatic proof that price will reverse.","An instruction to double the position."],"correct":0,"why":"The disagreement is an early clue, not the entry. The original move can continue."}},"5.4":{"title":"Bollinger Method IV: Wait for a Confirmed Breakout","timeLabel":"25–28 minutes","take":"Method IV asks for two outside closes after a squeeze, then uses ADX to decide whether to read the move as continuation or a potential reversal.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 5.4</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>What if you want more evidence than the first close outside a band?</p>\n<p>Bollinger&#39;s later Method IV, called <strong>Confirmed Breakouts</strong>, is a variation on Method I. Instead of alerting on the initial outside-band close, it waits for two consecutive closes beyond a band after a squeeze. It also uses the Average Directional Index, or ADX, to help decide whether the move is occurring in a trending or non-trending market.</p>\n<p>This method was developed after <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>. It should be taught as Bollinger&#39;s later extension, not as one of the book&#39;s original three methods.</p>\n<h4>The shared starting condition</h4>\n<p>First identify a squeeze using BandWidth. The official software manual uses a 125-period low as the default squeeze definition. Then watch for two consecutive closes outside the same band.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Upper-band case:</strong> two closes in a row above the upper band.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower-band case:</strong> two closes in a row below the lower band.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A wick beyond a band followed by a close back inside does not count as two consecutive outside closes. The definition is specific, so avoid quietly changing it when reviewing examples.</p>\n<h4>Why ADX changes the reading</h4>\n<p>ADX is used to estimate how strong a trend is. The Method IV manual uses an ADX threshold to distinguish a trending condition from a non-trending one. The threshold is configurable; the method documentation does not establish one number as universally correct for every market.</p>\n<p>This creates a useful but less obvious distinction:</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Confirmed move after a squeeze</th><th>ADX above the chosen threshold</th><th>ADX below the chosen threshold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Two closes above the upper band</td><td>Treat as a trend-following buy alert.</td><td>Treat as a potential reversal alert in the opposite direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Two closes below the lower band</td><td>Treat as a trend-following short alert.</td><td>Treat as a potential reversal alert in the opposite direction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>This is the method&#39;s central twist. A confirmed move outside a band is not interpreted the same way in every market condition. In a strong trend it can support continuation; in a non-trending market the same event can be read as a possible reversal setup.</p>\n<h4>From confirmed breakout to a trade plan</h4>\n<p>The two-close requirement answers a specific question—did price hold outside the same band for two closes?—but it can make the entry later. That delay is part of the confirmation design. Do not quietly move the entry earlier and still report it as the same rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Prepare:</strong> identify the squeeze and nearby range boundaries first. Set the ADX threshold as part of the implementation you are studying; it is configurable, not a universal number. Then wait for two consecutive closes outside the same band.</p>\n<p><strong>Continuation case:</strong> with ADX above the chosen threshold, two closes above the upper band create the long-continuation reading; two closes below the lower band create the short-continuation reading. A simple course execution plan is to enter after the second close if price is not extended and room remains. A more cautious variant enters only if the next bar breaks the second candle’s high for a long or low for a short. The latter is an added execution filter and can miss a move that immediately accelerates.</p>\n<p><strong>Potential reversal case:</strong> with ADX below the configured threshold, the later Method IV implementation reads the confirmed outside-band move as a potential reversal alert in the opposite direction. Do not take the continuation trade automatically. For a course execution rule, wait for price to break back through the confirmed move’s short-term swing (for example, below a two-close-up sequence’s local low for a possible short) rather than shorting just because ADX is low. This is an added confirmation choice and should be tested separately from the indicator’s alert.</p>\n<p><strong>Stop:</strong> for continuation longs, a stop below the second breakout bar’s low or below the latest swing/range retest is a structural choice; for continuation shorts use the corresponding high. For reversal entries, place the invalidation beyond the breakout extreme. The stop belongs where that specific interpretation becomes wrong. Size the position from the entry-to-stop distance; if the distance leaves too little reward to the next level, do not enter.</p>\n<p>The linked chart examples are for practicing breakout and head-fake reading. They must not automatically be labelled exact Method IV signals unless you can verify the squeeze, two consecutive outside closes, and the ADX state from the chart data.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Treating Method IV as “wait for any two large candles.” The rule concerns two consecutive closes outside the same Bollinger Band after a squeeze, with ADX used in the method&#39;s interpretation.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Method IV asks for two outside closes after a squeeze, then uses ADX to decide whether to read the move as continuation or a potential reversal.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/_files/ugd/58be43_d09c50b6e8ea4afd9af0523ef94de876.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bollinger Bands Confirmed Breakouts (Method IV) Manual</a>. This is Bollinger&#39;s later method, published after the first edition of his book.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Historical chart examples to compare</h4>\n<p>For every chart, count the outside-band closes yourself. Then ask whether ADX is above or below the threshold used in the method. These examples help train breakout-versus-head-fake reading; they do not all show the complete Method IV indicator set.</p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.4.1 — A squeeze followed by an upside expansion.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.4.2 — A historical compression and breakout sequence.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.4.3 — A breakout sequence to classify bar by bar.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.4.4 — A head-fake sequence—inspect where the first break fails.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.4.5 — Another false-start and later-resolution comparison.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Practice prompt:</strong> A chart with one close outside the band is not yet a two-close Method IV confirmation. Mark the second qualifying close, read ADX using the chosen threshold, then write a stop and entry before exposing yourself to the following bars.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: One close is not two-close confirmation</h4>\n<p>Assume the precondition for the selected Method IV variation has been met. Price closes above the upper band on one completed candle. That is only the first close; the two-close condition is not complete. If the next candle also closes above the same band, the required sequence is present and you can then assess the remaining source-defined conditions, including the ADX context specified by the method.</p>\n<p>In a different sequence, the second candle closes back inside the bands. The two consecutive outside closes have not occurred. A chart that shows one dramatic candle may look stronger than a quieter two-candle sequence, but visual size cannot replace the written rule. More confirmation can also mean a later entry and less remaining room before resistance. After checking the setup conditions, do a separate stop-distance and trade-off assessment rather than assuming that confirmation guarantees continuation.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Inspect Figure 5.4</h4>\n<p>Compare a single close outside a band with two consecutive closes. Then compare the ADX contexts. Figure 5.4 is designed to show that “confirmed breakout” describes a specific condition and that the method&#39;s interpretation also depends on the regime filter.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> In each panel, identify whether the confirmed-breakout condition occurred and whether the method classifies it as trend-following or reversal under the ADX state shown.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> Two consecutive outside closes after a squeeze meet the confirmation condition. ADX then determines whether the official Method IV interpretation is continuation or the opposite-direction reversal alert. If the two-close condition is missing, do not label it a confirmed breakout under this method.</p>","quiz":{"question":"What does the two-close condition require in Method IV?","choices":["One candle with a long body beyond either band.","Two consecutive completed closes beyond the same outer band, with the remaining source-defined conditions checked.","Two candles that touch different outer bands."],"correct":1,"why":"A single outside close does not satisfy a two-consecutive-close rule. Context and the independent trade plan still matter."}},"5.5":{"title":"Trade the W-Bottom and M-Top, Not Just the Shape","timeLabel":"25–28 minutes","take":"For a W or M pattern, read each swing relative to the bands, then wait for the price behaviour that confirms the trade idea.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 5.5</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>When does a W-bottom or M-top become more than a shape you can label after the event?</p>\n<p>John Bollinger devotes separate chapters to W-type bottoms and M-type tops. His distinctive contribution is to read the pattern&#39;s highs and lows relative to the bands, rather than judging only whether the second low or high is numerically higher or lower than the first.</p>\n<h4>The W-bottom long setup</h4>\n<p>Bollinger&#39;s classic W-bottom often begins with a decline that puts the first low at or below the lower band. Price then rallies and later retests the low area. The important detail is that the second low should occur inside the lower band, meaning it is a higher low on a <strong>relative</strong> basis. The second price low can still be lower in absolute price terms.</p>\n<p>Volume can add another clue: in Bollinger&#39;s example, the first decline has stronger volume than the second, and volume expands again as price lifts away from the pattern.</p>\n<p>The book gives a concrete entry idea: after a W that fits the rules, <strong>buy strength</strong>. Wait for a rally day whose range and volume are both above their recent averages. That day acts as confirmation rather than buying simply because the second low has appeared.</p>\n<p>For risk, Bollinger describes placing the stop below the most recent low on the right side of the W, then raising it as the trade develops. He discusses either a Parabolic-style approach or moving the stop below later consolidations and pullbacks. The target still needs to be considered before entry; do not assume every W must reach the upper band.</p>\n<h4>The M-top short setup</h4>\n<p>Tops often take longer to form than bottoms. Bollinger describes a common pattern in which an early push moves outside the upper band, a later push reaches the band again, and a subsequent push fails to reach the upper band even if price makes a marginal new high. Volume may also fade across the pushes.</p>\n<p>Do not short the first upper-band tag. Look for the pattern to develop and wait for a sign of weakness. Bollinger suggests looking for a down day with above-average range and volume. In a larger topping formation, a break below a meaningful swing low or neckline can be followed by a pullback that provides a clearer short entry; the risk can then be placed just above the pullback high.</p>\n<p>The sell-side pattern is not an exact mirror of the W-bottom. Tops often take more time and can involve three or more pushes. A pair of highs alone does not meet the full idea.</p>\n<h4>From pattern to entry, stop and target</h4>\n<p><strong>W-bottom long:</strong> the first low usually reaches or crosses the lower band. After a bounce, the second low should sit at a better relative position against the lower band, even if its absolute price is marginally lower. A weaker second decline can support the reading, but the entry is not simply “buy low two.” Bollinger’s example is to buy strength—such as the first strong up day with range and volume above recent averages. A course variant can wait for a break above that confirmation bar’s high.</p>\n<p>Place invalidation below the right-side low. If the low is too distant, the correct response is to reduce size or skip, not to move the stop into ordinary price noise. Look toward the intervening high or upper part of the range as a possible obstacle/target, but accept the trade only if reward relative to risk is sufficient.</p>\n<p><strong>M-top short:</strong> the first push may reach or exceed the upper band; after a pullback, a later push may make a marginal new price high but fail to reach the upper band. Wait for a sign of weakness, such as a down day with above-average range and volume. A more conservative course variant waits for a break under the intervening swing low, then uses a pullback that fails as a short trigger. Put invalidation above the pullback high or top extreme, matching the exact entry you chose.</p>\n<p><strong>Do not force symmetry:</strong> tops may take more pushes and can remain strong for longer than a bottoming formation. If the later push still reaches the upper band with strong upward progress, the evidence for a weakening M-top is incomplete. If support does not break, do not invent a short confirmation.</p>\n<p><strong>No-trade filter:</strong> no strong confirmation, a clear trend continuing in the original direction, an entry too far from the stop, or too little space before the next barrier are valid reasons to pass.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>For a W or M pattern, read each swing relative to the bands, then wait for the price behaviour that confirms the trade idea.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger, <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>, Chapters 12 and 13; <a href=\"https://www.mheducation.com/highered/mhp/product/bollinger-bollinger-bands\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">book and chapter list from McGraw Hill</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>When the setup fails</h4>\n<p>For the long W, a second low that falls outside the lower band or makes a new relative low does not meet the classic band-based W description. A further decline can still follow. Do not rescue the pattern by changing the definition after the fact.</p>\n<p>For the short M-type top, repeated pushes that continue to reach the upper band with strong price and volume progress do not yet show the weakening the setup looks for. If price breaks higher and holds, the short idea should be cancelled or reassessed under the plan.</p>\n<h4>Historical chart examples to compare</h4>\n<p>Look first at the position of each swing relative to the bands. Then mark the confirmation and invalidation levels; do not judge the pattern by the W/M shape alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.5.1 — A W-bottom with a second low that improves relative to the lower band.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.5.2 — A second W-bottom for comparison.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.5.3 — An M-type top with a later weakening relationship to the upper band.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.5.4 — Another M-type top to study for the confirmation point.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.5.5 — A W-bottom-shaped reversal and its later move.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.5.6 — A bearish reversal-shaped pattern.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Practice prompt:</strong> For each panel, circle the swing that changes the interpretation, mark the trigger and stop as if you were trading live, and identify the moment at which the setup is still incomplete.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: A complete pattern still needs a viable trade</h4>\n<p>Suppose a W-bottom&#39;s first low falls outside the lower band and its second low forms inside the band at a higher relative position. Price then breaks the intervening swing high, meeting the confirmation rule chosen for the exercise. If the logical invalidation is below the second low, measure the distance from a possible entry to that point and compare it with the next resistance area. The pattern may be valid yet still offer a poor trade.</p>\n<p>For the mirrored M-top, compare the two highs, wait for the stated downside confirmation and define where the bearish idea is wrong. In either direction, do not move the stop closer simply to improve the reward-to-risk ratio on paper. If the structural stop is too far away or the next obstacle is too close, record “valid pattern, no trade.” Setup quality and trade suitability are separate judgements.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Task</h4>\n<p>In Figure 5.5, identify the relative second low in the W-bottom and the weakening final push in the M-top. Mark the confirmation day for the long and the evidence of weakness for the short. Then identify one example that does <strong>not</strong> qualify.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The W-bottom requires improving relative position on the retest, ideally with weaker volume on the second decline, followed by a strong up day with above-average range and volume. The M-top needs the later push to show weaker relative price action and then a sign of weakness. A shape with no confirmation—or one that continues to make strong progress in the original direction—does not qualify.</p>\n<h4>Try a new case</h4>\n<p>The second low in a possible W-bottom is lower than the first in price, but it is now inside the lower band rather than outside it. Volume on the retest is also lower. Is the pattern automatically invalid because the price low is lower?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> No. Bollinger&#39;s approach compares the lows relative to the bands. The second low can be lower in absolute price while still being a higher low relative to the bands. The setup still needs the planned confirmation and risk assessment.</p>","quiz":{"question":"A W-bottom is confirmed, but the stop below the second low leaves little room to the next obstacle. What is the best conclusion?","choices":["Confirmation guarantees that the target will be reached.","Tighten the stop regardless of the pattern structure.","The setup may be valid, but the trade can still be unsuitable."],"correct":2,"why":"Confirmation does not remove the need for a viable entry, structural invalidation and adequate room."}},"5.6":{"title":"John Carter's TTM Squeeze: Use Momentum for Direction","timeLabel":"25–28 minutes","take":"Carter's TTM Squeeze uses the relationship between two volatility envelopes, then uses momentum to help choose direction. The release is not a guarantee.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 5.6</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>How is John Carter&#39;s TTM Squeeze different from Bollinger&#39;s own Squeeze setup?</p>\n<p>Both focus on quiet conditions that may be followed by a wider move, but they define the squeeze differently.</p>\n<p>Bollinger&#39;s BandWidth approach compares the current width of Bollinger Bands with their own history. Carter&#39;s TTM Squeeze compares Bollinger Bands with Keltner Channels. In the common TTM implementation, a squeeze is “on” when the Bollinger Bands fit inside the Keltner Channels. It “fires” when the Bollinger Bands expand back outside them.</p>\n<p>A momentum histogram is then used to help assess direction. This is a different method from simply waiting for price to close outside a Bollinger Band.</p>\n<h4>The long setup</h4>\n<p>A common implementation uses 20-period Bollinger Bands with two standard deviations and 20-period Keltner Channels with an ATR multiplier of 1.5. These are conventional settings, not laws. Check the definition used by the charting platform because implementations can differ.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Observe:</strong> The Bollinger Bands are inside the Keltner Channels for a period. Volatility is compressed under this definition.</li>\n<li><strong>Interpret:</strong> The market may be preparing for a wider move, but compression alone says nothing about whether it will be upward or downward.</li>\n<li><strong>Test:</strong> Wait for the squeeze to fire. For a long setup under the common implementation, the momentum histogram is above zero and rising, indicating strengthening upward momentum. A trader may also require price to clear a nearby range boundary, but that extra condition must be identified as an added filter rather than part of the squeeze definition itself.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>The short setup</h4>\n<p>The short-side reading is the mirror: the squeeze fires and the momentum histogram is below zero and falling, indicating strengthening downward momentum. Again, the compression alone is not the direction signal; the momentum reading and price behaviour supply that part of the decision.</p>\n<h4>From squeeze release to entry and risk</h4>\n<p><strong>Before the release:</strong> the TTM squeeze is on while the Bollinger Bands remain inside the Keltner Channels. That is a prepare/watch condition, not an entry by itself. Mark the compression range and recent swing levels while the squeeze is on. A trader who enters before the release is anticipating the setup; label and test that as a separate strategy.</p>\n<p><strong>Trigger and entry:</strong> under the common implementation, wait for the squeeze to fire as the bands expand back outside the Keltner Channels. For a long, the momentum histogram should be above zero and rising; for a short, below zero and falling. A repeatable course execution rule enters on the first completed fire bar that also meets the chosen momentum condition. A more cautious variant waits for the next bar to break the fire bar’s high/low. It can miss sharp moves, but the rule is clear and testable.</p>\n<p><strong>Stop:</strong> use the compression boundary or the swing low/high that invalidates the breakout thesis. A long stop below the lower edge of the compressed range gives the idea more room than a stop just below the trigger candle, but it needs a smaller position. For a short, mirror the level. If price has already travelled so far that the stop or next barrier makes the trade unattractive, let the move go.</p>\n<p><strong>Exit:</strong> a common TTM implementation reduces or exits when histogram momentum begins to fade toward zero; some implementations use two consecutive bars of weakening colour. Treat this as a defined management variant, not a promise or a mandatory rule across every platform. Do not change exit logic midway through a losing trade.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure clues:</strong> the fire occurs but price returns to the compression range, momentum fails to expand in the chosen direction, or the first move immediately reverses. Follow the stop/exit you selected before entry. A release gives no guarantee that a durable trend will follow.</p>\n<h4>Do not mix up the two squeeze approaches</h4>\n<p>Bollinger BandWidth Squeeze: narrow Bollinger Bands relative to their own past, then look for a price breakout.</p>\n<p>TTM Squeeze: Bollinger Bands inside Keltner Channels, then watch for the release and use the momentum component to help judge direction.</p>\n<p>Both may be useful setups to study. They are not the same calculation, and a result from one should not be presented as evidence that the other works identically.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Carter&#39;s TTM Squeeze uses the relationship between two volatility envelopes, then uses momentum to help choose direction. The release is not a guarantee.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John F. Carter, <em>Mastering the Trade</em>, Third Edition; <a href=\"https://windsorpublishing.com/product/mastering-the-tradethird-edition/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Windsor Books publisher page</a>. For the common calculation and momentum-reading convention, see <a href=\"https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-indicators/ttm-squeeze\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">StockCharts&#39; TTM Squeeze reference</a>.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>## Source note for this module</p>\n<p>John Bollinger&#39;s three book methods are documented in Chapters 16, 19 and 20 of <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>. Method IV is a later official extension, documented in Bollinger&#39;s own TradeStation manual. Carter&#39;s TTM Squeeze is a separate method. The setup conditions above are attributed to the relevant method; where the course adds a boundary, risk filter or execution choice, it must be treated as an additional rule to test rather than part of the author&#39;s original setup.</p>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Historical chart examples to compare</h4>\n<p>Read the squeeze state and the momentum histogram together. A green/fire state by itself is not a direction signal; mark whether the histogram is above zero and increasing or below zero and decreasing.</p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.6.1 — A bullish TTM squeeze release with the momentum pane.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.6.2 — A squeeze chart with price and momentum for a second case.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.6.3 — A more recent squeeze-release chart to classify.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.6.4 — A bearish squeeze case with the channel relationship and momentum.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.6.5 — A TTM-style chart showing squeeze state and upward momentum.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Practice prompt:</strong> For every example, record the first fire bar, histogram sign and direction, candidate entry, stop boundary, and first obstacle. Also look for a release that fails; do not count compression as a trade.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: The squeeze state and the momentum reading answer different questions</h4>\n<p>In a possible long case, the Bollinger Bands contract inside the Keltner Channels, so the TTM squeeze condition is active under the selected settings. The momentum histogram is positive and starts to strengthen as the bands release. That combination can support monitoring a long scenario, but price still needs to meet the trigger in your plan. The release and positive momentum do not guarantee follow-through.</p>\n<p>In the contrasting case, the same compression resolves downward and the momentum reading is negative. The setup question changes, but risk logic does not: identify the short trigger, invalidation and next support area before entry. If the momentum histogram turns while price remains trapped in the range, the evidence may be mixed. Avoid treating a coloured histogram or a squeeze-release marker as an automatic order; record the actual price event and apply the rule consistently.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Task</h4>\n<p>Use Figure 5.6 to mark (a) when the TTM squeeze is on, (b) when it fires, and (c) which panel gives a long or short momentum reading. Identify a failed release if one is shown.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The squeeze is on while the Bollinger Bands remain inside the Keltner Channels. The fire occurs when they expand back outside. A rising positive histogram fits the long example; a falling negative histogram fits the short example. If price immediately fails the breakout or momentum weakens, the original plan needs to be reassessed.</p>\n<h4>Try a new case</h4>\n<p>The TTM squeeze fires, but the histogram is below zero and rising toward zero. Should you enter long just because the squeeze released?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> Not on that evidence alone. The histogram is below zero and rising, so downward momentum may be easing, but it has not shown the common positive-and-rising long condition. I would wait for the direction and price trigger required by the chosen method or skip the trade.</p>","quiz":{"question":"Which statement correctly separates the TTM Squeeze from BandWidth?","choices":["TTM compares Bollinger Bands with Keltner Channels; BandWidth measures the band span relative to the middle band.","They always use identical definitions and settings.","The momentum histogram defines Bollinger BandWidth."],"correct":0,"why":"The squeeze state and the momentum reading answer different questions. Neither removes the need to assess the actual price trigger."}},"6.1":{"title":"The False Start After a Squeeze","timeLabel":"16 minutes","take":"A failed breakout is evidence that the original idea may no longer fit—not an automatic signal to trade the other way.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 6.1</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>What if price breaks upward after a squeeze, then quickly falls back into the prior range?</p>\n<p>A quiet period can be followed by a stronger move, but not every first move lasts. Bollinger discusses the possibility of <strong>head-fakes</strong> around squeezes: an initial move in one direction can fail before price develops a clearer move. The term names a type of failure; it does not mean it can always be forecast in advance.</p>\n<h4>The danger of treating the first break as proof</h4>\n<p>Imagine price has been moving in a narrow consolidation. Your plan says a completed close above the range high is the trigger for a potential long. Price closes above it. This is evidence that the trigger occurred. It is not proof that the breakout will continue.</p>\n<p>The next bar returns inside the prior range and price cannot regain the boundary under your chosen confirmation rule. Now the evidence has changed. If your plan required a hold above the boundary, the condition has failed. The correct response is to cancel or manage the original idea according to the plan, not keep insisting that price must recover.</p>\n<p>A failed upside break does not automatically become a short. The original long condition has weakened, but a short needs its own context, trigger, invalidation, stop, and target. If the downside move has not produced those conditions, waiting is still reasonable.</p>\n<h4>Plan the failure before the entry</h4>\n<p>For each squeeze-breakout idea, write down:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What boundary must price cross?</li>\n<li>What counts as the trigger?</li>\n<li>Does the method require a hold or another confirmation?</li>\n<li>What event invalidates the breakout idea?</li>\n<li>If it fails, what evidence would be needed before considering the opposite direction?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Defining failure in advance helps you avoid changing the rules to fit the latest candle.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Reversing position immediately after a failed breakout without waiting for a new plan.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>A failed breakout is evidence that the original idea may no longer fit—not an automatic signal to trade the other way.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> The squeeze and head-fake concept is associated with John Bollinger&#39;s <em>Bollinger on Bollinger Bands</em>, particularly the Squeeze discussion. The invalidation rule used here is an example chosen for teaching. See <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bollinger&#39;s official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Inspect Figure 6.1</h4>\n<p>Find the first close beyond the range. Then locate the return inside. The key question is not “Was the breakout fake from the start?” That answer is usually unknowable. Ask instead: “At what point did the price behaviour fail the conditions of the plan?”</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: Define what counts as failure before the breakout</h4>\n<p>Price compresses between 100 and 104, then closes at 105. A plan that requires a second close above the upper band may not be active yet; a plan that enters on the first completed close may be active but must already have an invalidation rule. The following candle falls below 104 and returns to 102. Depending on the written plan, this may invalidate the breakout or trigger a managed exit.</p>\n<p>Now compare a chart where price closes above 104, holds above the boundary and continues making higher highs. The first candle alone did not distinguish the cases. The difference emerges in what happens next and in the conditions the plan required. After a failed break, do not immediately sell. A separate short setup needs its own price trigger and risk plan; otherwise the failure simply means the original long idea is no longer supported.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Test: Locate the failure point</h4>\n<p>Mark the initial trigger, the evidence that weakens the continuation idea, and the point at which the chosen rule says to stop treating it as valid.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The first close beyond the boundary meets the trigger chosen for this example. The quick return inside and failure to hold weaken the continuation case. If the plan specified that this behaviour invalidates the setup, the original plan should be cancelled. No reverse trade should be assumed without a separate trigger.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>Price breaks down after a squeeze, then quickly recovers above the lower boundary of the prior range. What is the mirror-image lesson?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> The downside breakout may have failed. The original short condition should be reviewed against its invalidation rule. A long requires its own evidence; it is not automatic simply because the short idea weakened.</p>","quiz":{"question":"A breakout returns quickly into the old range. What follows automatically?","choices":["A trade in the opposite direction.","Nothing automatically; apply the original plan's invalidation and reassess new evidence.","A larger position on the original side."],"correct":1,"why":"The failed move weakens the original thesis; it does not create a short or long signal by itself."}},"6.2":{"title":"When a Band Walk Traps Reversal Traders","timeLabel":"15 minutes","take":"Before fading a band walk, look for a real change in price behaviour. A feeling that price is stretched is not enough.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 6.2</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>What evidence warns you that price may keep moving along the band instead of reversing?</p>\n<p>A common failure is to decide that price has moved “too far” simply because it touches the upper band repeatedly. In a strong uptrend, price can walk the upper band and keep moving higher. In a strong downtrend, it can walk the lower band and keep moving lower.</p>\n<h4>Read the progress, not just the location</h4>\n<p>Compare three things:</p>\n<p><strong>Band interaction:</strong> Is price touching the same band once, or repeatedly over a sequence?</p>\n<p><strong>Swing structure:</strong> Are highs and lows still progressing in the trend direction, or are the swings starting to fail?</p>\n<p><strong>Middle band:</strong> Does it continue to slope in the trend direction, or has it flattened or changed? This is supporting context, not a stand-alone signal.</p>\n<p>A persistent upper-band walk alongside rising swing highs and lows is different from a chart where price repeatedly touches the upper band but fails to make further progress. The second situation may deserve closer examination for a change in behaviour, but it still does not guarantee reversal.</p>\n<h4>Why mean reversion can fail</h4>\n<p>A mean-reversion idea assumes that price may return toward the middle or move away from an extreme. That idea can struggle when a strong trend continues. If you keep selling the upper band during an uptrend, the trade may be stopped repeatedly while price walks higher. If you keep buying the lower band during a downtrend, the same problem can occur in reverse.</p>\n<p>Before taking a reversal, identify what would show that the current trend is weakening or structure is changing. That could involve a break of a meaningful swing, a failure to make further progress, or a clearly defined pattern confirmation. Choose the rule before the outcome, and keep it consistent while testing.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Confusing “price is far from the average” with “price must reverse now.”</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Before fading a band walk, look for a real change in price behaviour. A feeling that price is stretched is not enough.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> John Bollinger&#39;s discussion of walking the bands and Official Rule 7. <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Inspect Figure 6.2</h4>\n<p>Panel A shows persistent directional progress alongside repeated band interaction. Panel B shows a walk where the structure begins to weaken. Notice that “beginning to weaken” is not the same as “reversal confirmed.” You still need the chosen trigger.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: A lower-band touch inside a downtrend</h4>\n<p>Price falls from 120 to 114, rebounds to 116 and then moves to 110. Several closes cluster near the lower band, while each rebound fails below the prior swing high. The lower-band location may look stretched, but the sequence still shows downside progress. Buying because “it cannot go lower” would ignore the evidence in the swings.</p>\n<p>In a second chart, price tags the lower band, fails to make another meaningful low, reclaims a prior swing level and begins forming higher lows. That is a different set of facts and may justify evaluating a reversal setup if it meets the plan&#39;s conditions. It still does not guarantee a sustained recovery. The useful distinction is the change in structure, not merely the number of times price touched the band. If no change appears, waiting is more defensible than fading the trend.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Test: Decide whether to fade the band walk</h4>\n<p>Which panel is less suitable for fading the trend immediately? Point to two facts that support your decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The panel with ongoing directional progress and intact swing structure is less suitable for a countertrend trade. Repeated band interaction is part of the trend context, not proof of overextension. The weakening panel may be worth watching but still requires a clear trigger.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>In a downtrend, price has closed near the lower band for five bars and made a fresh low. Is the fifth touch a long signal?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> No. The chart still shows directional progress down. The trader needs evidence of a change in behaviour and a planned trigger before considering a long.</p>","quiz":{"question":"Price repeatedly closes near the lower band while swing highs and lows keep falling. Which reading is better supported?","choices":["Price must be cheap and should be bought.","A bullish divergence is confirmed by the band touches.","A downside band walk may be underway; a reversal needs separate evidence."],"correct":2,"why":"Persistent downside progress warns against fading the trend based only on relative price location."}},"6.3":{"title":"When a Range Trade Stops Being a Range Trade","timeLabel":"15 minutes","take":"When the condition that justified your range trade no longer holds, stop managing the trade as if it still does.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 6.3</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>If price crosses a range boundary and continues moving away, what happens to a plan that expected a bounce?</p>\n<p>A range-fade trade depends on the range continuing to hold. If price begins to stay beyond the boundary and make directional progress, the original premise may no longer fit.</p>\n<p>The challenge is that a brief move outside the range can happen before price returns inside. That is why a single wick beyond the boundary and a sustained break should not automatically be treated as the same event.</p>\n<h4>Separate a false poke from a changing market</h4>\n<p>In the first panel of Figure 6.3, price moves beyond the boundary but returns into the range. If your plan required a close or hold outside the boundary to invalidate the range, the first move may not have met that condition. You still need to follow the exact rule you selected.</p>\n<p>In the second panel, price moves beyond the boundary, remains there under the stated example rule, and begins progressing away. If the range-fade plan said that acceptance outside the boundary invalidates the idea, the original trade is no longer valid once the condition occurs.</p>\n<p>You do not have to label the event perfectly in real time. The purpose of a rule is to define what you will do when the observed behaviour no longer supports your premise.</p>\n<h4>Do not defend the old plan</h4>\n<p>A frequent mistake is to average into a losing range trade because price “should come back.” But the range was an assumption based on past behaviour. It is not a promise. When price violates the invalidation condition, your plan needs to change—usually by closing or managing the trade according to the rules, then reassessing from a neutral position.</p>\n<p>A new breakout trade may become possible, but it is a separate decision. It needs a new trigger and a sensible risk/reward. Do not turn a failed fade into an impulsive breakout entry just to recover the loss.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Treating a trading range as permanent because it has held several times before.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>When the condition that justified your range trade no longer holds, stop managing the trade as if it still does.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> Bollinger&#39;s rules warn against treating band tags as signals and emphasise setup-based use. The range-break and acceptance examples are general market-structure rules used here, not a Bollinger-specific named system.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Inspect Figure 6.3</h4>\n<p>Mark the prior range high/low, the first excursion beyond the boundary, and the later behaviour. Ask what the original plan required and where that condition was met or failed.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: When the old range thesis no longer fits</h4>\n<p>A range has repeatedly turned near 104, and a trader is watching an upper-band tag for a possible short back toward the middle. Price instead closes above 104 with a broad candle and the next period holds above the former ceiling. The old range-reversal premise has been challenged. Continuing to expect the same bounce because it worked before is not evidence; it is attachment to the prior idea.</p>\n<p>Contrast that with a brief wick above 104 that closes back inside and is followed by a return toward the middle of the range. That behaviour may fit a failed probe, although the short still needs its own trigger. The two cases should not be classified just by the first excursion. Decide which close/hold condition your method uses, then apply it consistently. When the boundary has changed role or the range premise has failed, reassess instead of defending the old target.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Test: Check whether the range premise still holds</h4>\n<p>For each panel, explain whether the range-fade premise remains intact under the example rule. State one thing you would do if the premise is invalidated.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> In the false-poke example, price returns into the prior range and may not have met the stated invalidation condition. In the sustained-break example, price remains outside and progresses, so the range premise is weakened or invalidated under the rule. The trader should stop treating the old fade as valid and avoid adding risk to defend it.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>You are short at the top of a range. Price closes above the range high twice and then pulls back toward it. What should happen next?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> If the plan defined the sustained closes above the boundary as invalidation, the short premise has failed. The pullback may later offer a breakout-retest setup, but that requires a separate plan; it should not be used to justify holding the old short.</p>","quiz":{"question":"Price breaks above a range ceiling and then holds beyond it. How should a prior range-fade plan be treated?","choices":["Reassess or invalidate it under the written rule rather than defending the old target.","Keep the short open because the upper band should resist price.","Assume every boundary break is false."],"correct":0,"why":"A boundary hold can contradict the old range premise. The new state needs its own analysis rather than attachment to the old idea."}},"6.4":{"title":"Late Entries, Conflicting Evidence, and No-Trade Conditions","timeLabel":"16 minutes","take":"A good setup is not just a direction. It also needs a suitable entry, a clear point of failure, manageable risk, and enough room to make sense.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 6.4</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>What if the breakout is real, but most of the move has already happened?</p>\n<p>Being right about direction does not guarantee that a trade at the current price makes sense. If price has already travelled far from the original boundary, the stop may need to be wide and the next target may be close. The remaining opportunity can be poor even if the overall trend continues.</p>\n<h4>Four reasons to wait</h4>\n<p><strong>1. The move is late.</strong> The bands have already widened and price has moved far beyond the original setup area. Chasing may create a poor entry relative to the logical invalidation point. You do not have to participate in every move.</p>\n<p><strong>2. The evidence conflicts.</strong> Price may be testing a boundary while the swing structure remains unclear, or the selected confirmation may disagree with the price action. Conflicting evidence does not always mean the market will reverse. It may mean you do not yet have a clear trade.</p>\n<p><strong>3. The instrument or conditions are unsuitable.</strong> Thin liquidity, large spreads, gaps, sudden event-driven movement, or unreliable data can make the chart-based stop and the actual fill behave differently. Bollinger Bands do not account for these risks. A chart alone may not show the full execution problem.</p>\n<p><strong>4. There is too little room to target.</strong> If the next major price area is close while invalidation is far away, the trade may offer too little potential reward for its planned loss. Do not squeeze the stop into an arbitrary position just to make the ratio look better.</p>\n<h4>No-trade is a decision, not a lack of skill</h4>\n<p>A useful no-trade statement says why the setup does not qualify. For example: “The breakout has already expanded too far from the boundary; the nearest resistance leaves too little room before the planned target.” That is more useful than “I don&#39;t like it.”</p>\n<p>A no-trade condition should be concrete enough to apply consistently. Examples include: the required trigger has not occurred; price structure is mixed; the original premise is invalidated; execution conditions are poor; or the stop and target do not produce acceptable planned risk.</p>\n<p>No single checklist can make every situation safe. The point is to avoid taking a trade when the conditions you said you required are missing.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Believing that missing a move is worse than entering a low-quality trade.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>A good setup is not just a direction. It also needs a suitable entry, a clear point of failure, manageable risk, and enough room to make sense.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> Bollinger&#39;s Official Rule 22 says bands identify setups rather than provide continuous advice. The execution and reward-to-risk filters here are general course risk principles. <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Inspect Figure 6.4</h4>\n<p>Each panel is a reason to pause, but for a different reason. Identify whether the problem is location, disagreement, execution risk, or insufficient room to target.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: A real move can still be a poor entry</h4>\n<p>Price breaks above 104 and travels quickly to 110 while the bands widen. If a trader waits until 109 to enter, places the invalidation at 105 and sees a potential resistance area near 111, the setup has only about 2 points of nearby room for roughly 4 points of stop distance before costs. The breakout may be real, but the trade-off is unattractive.</p>\n<p>A different setup breaks above 104, holds near 105 and has clear room toward 111 while its structural invalidation sits near 102. That case may deserve assessment because entry, risk and available room have not deteriorated in the same way. Neither example proves profitability. It shows why “direction correct” and “entry worthwhile” are different questions. Waiting for a better location or skipping the trade are decisions, not missed obligations.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Identify reasons to wait</h4>\n<p>Choose which cases should be skipped and write the specific reason. Do not use only “it looks risky”; identify the visible condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> A late move may have poor entry location; conflicting evidence may leave the thesis unresolved; gaps or poor liquidity may make execution risk hard to control; and insufficient room to target can make the planned reward unattractive. Any one may justify waiting under the trader&#39;s rules.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>The bands are wide and price is moving strongly upward. The breakout itself is clear, but price is far from the original boundary and just below a prior high. Do you have to buy?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> No. The trend may be strong, but the entry may be late and the nearby high may limit the available target. I can wait for a better setup or skip it.</p>","quiz":{"question":"The move is already extended and the next obstacle offers less room than the stop distance. What is the best decision?","choices":["Chase immediately because the direction is obvious.","Pass or wait for a better location; a real move can still be a poor trade.","Move the stop closer without reference to structure."],"correct":1,"why":"Direction alone does not make an entry worthwhile. Include stop distance, costs, slippage and room to the next obstacle."}},"7.1":{"title":"Size the Trade From the Point Where the Idea Fails","timeLabel":"16 minutes","take":"First decide where the idea fails. Then calculate a position size that fits the loss you are prepared to risk.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 7.1</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>Why should the stop be decided before you work out the position size?</p>\n<p>Your stop should represent the point where the trade idea no longer fits your plan. The distance from entry to that stop helps determine how many units, shares, contracts, or lots you can trade while keeping the planned loss within a chosen limit.</p>\n<p>This is different from picking a convenient position size first and then placing the stop where the number looks comfortable.</p>\n<h4>A simple calculation</h4>\n<p>For a straightforward instrument where the profit/loss per price unit is known:</p>\n<p><strong>Position size = allowed cash risk ÷ planned loss per unit</strong></p>\n<p>The planned loss per unit is usually based on the distance between entry and stop, multiplied by the instrument&#39;s value per price unit. Real instruments may require additional contract multipliers, currency conversions, lot specifications, fees, and other adjustments. Use the correct contract details for the market you trade.</p>\n<p>For example, suppose a trader decides that a planned loss of ₹1,000 is the maximum for a particular idea. If the entry-to-stop distance would lose ₹10 per unit, the position size would be 100 units before costs and execution adjustments. If a different setup needs a stop that would lose ₹20 per unit, the same cash-risk limit would allow 50 units. The wider stop means a smaller position if planned cash risk stays the same.</p>\n<p>These numbers are a calculation example, not a recommendation for how much any trader should risk.</p>\n<h4>The stop is not a guarantee</h4>\n<p>A stop order does not guarantee an exit at the exact stop price. A gap, fast market, slippage, poor liquidity, or trading halt can produce a worse result. In markets with contract sizes or leveraged exposure, a small price move may have a larger cash effect than expected. Check the instrument&#39;s mechanics before applying the simple formula.</p>\n<p>The Bollinger Bands do not decide your risk allowance. The bands can help you read the market condition, but your invalidation point comes from the trade idea and price structure. The position size follows from the risk you choose to accept.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Using the same position size on every setup even when the stop distance changes.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>First decide where the idea fails. Then calculate a position size that fits the loss you are prepared to risk.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> This is general risk-management teaching, not a position-sizing rule prescribed by John Bollinger. Adapt the arithmetic to the instrument&#39;s contract specifications, costs, and account currency.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Read Figure 7.1</h4>\n<p>Compare the two examples. They use the same allowed cash risk but different entry-to-stop distances. Notice how the wider stop corresponds to a smaller position under the simplified calculation.</p>\n<h4>Worked calculation: Let the stop distance determine size</h4>\n<p>Imagine an account of ₹2,00,000 and a chosen maximum planned risk of 0.5%, or ₹1,000. A possible entry is ₹250 and the structural stop is ₹242, so the planned distance is ₹8 per share. The arithmetic gives ₹1,000 ÷ ₹8 = 125 shares before transaction costs and slippage. A cautious plan may round down further to leave a buffer. The example is a sizing calculation, not a recommendation to trade that instrument.</p>\n<p>If the structural stop were at ₹234, the distance would be ₹16 and the same cash-risk limit would allow at most 62 shares before costs. Do not move the stop to ₹242 just to permit a larger position if ₹234 is where the structure actually invalidates the idea. For futures or contracts, include the lot size and point value in the calculation. Gaps and slippage can cause actual loss to exceed the planned amount.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and calculate: Work out the position size</h4>\n<p>In Figure 7.1, calculate the position size for each example using the given allowed cash risk and per-unit loss. Then state one reason the realised loss may differ from the planned amount.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> Divide the permitted cash loss by the planned loss per unit. The wider stop allows fewer units for the same planned risk. Actual loss may be higher because of gaps, slippage, fees, or incorrect assumptions about the instrument&#39;s value.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>Two traders have the same account and same planned cash risk. One has a stop twice as far away as the other. If all other instrument details are the same, what happens to the position size?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> The trader using the wider stop should generally use about half the size to keep the same planned cash risk, before costs and execution differences.</p>","quiz":{"question":"If planned cash risk is ₹1,000 and entry-to-stop distance is ₹8 per share, what is the simple maximum size before costs?","choices":["8 shares.","1,000 shares.","125 shares."],"correct":2,"why":"₹1,000 divided by ₹8 is 125 shares before transaction costs, slippage and any further buffer."}},"7.2":{"title":"Decide How You Will Manage the Trade Before Entry","timeLabel":"15 minutes","take":"Choose management rules that fit the setup, and write them before price tests your discipline.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 7.2</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>If price moves in your favour, how will you know whether to hold, reduce, or exit?</p>\n<p>A plan should explain not only when to enter but how you will manage what happens next. Without a rule, each new candle can tempt you to improvise. You may exit too early because a candle looks worrying, or stay too long because you hope the move will continue.</p>\n<p>There is no single exit that suits every Bollinger Bands setup. A breakout plan and a range-fade plan are based on different ideas, so their exits may be different too.</p>\n<h4>Match the exit to the reason for entry</h4>\n<p><strong>Target-based exit:</strong> Before entry, identify a price area where the move may meet an obstacle or where your method expects to take profit. The target should be plausible relative to the stop distance. A target is a plan, not a promise that price will get there.</p>\n<p><strong>Structure-based management:</strong> If your approach follows a trend, you may choose to manage the trade using later swing structure. For example, an advance may remain intact while price forms higher lows. A break of the structure used by the plan may be a reason to exit. The exact rule needs to be stated and tested.</p>\n<p><strong>Rule-based exit from a weakening premise:</strong> You might decide in advance that a breakout plan is no longer valid if price returns into the prior range under the method&#39;s conditions. This is not the same as changing your mind because a candle feels uncomfortable. It follows a defined condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Time or no-progress exit:</strong> Some strategies are based on a move occurring within a reasonable period. If the move does not develop as expected, a prewritten time or no-progress rule may be appropriate. This is another rule to test, not something all traders should apply.</p>\n<h4>Why the middle band is not a universal exit</h4>\n<p>A crossing of the middle band can be informative in a particular method, but it is not automatically a universal exit or entry. In a range, price may cross it often. In a trend, it may act as a rough reference for pullbacks, but the outcome remains uncertain. If you want to use a middle-band cross, define exactly how and test it with the setup where it belongs.</p>\n<h4>Plan the paths before they occur</h4>\n<p>Figure 7.2 shows three possible paths after entry: price reaches the planned target, price reaches the invalidation condition, or price moves without resolving the idea. Each path should lead to an action that was selected before the outcome. That helps keep the management process consistent.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Deciding exits from emotion after entry rather than from the trade&#39;s original reason.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Choose management rules that fit the setup, and write them before price tests your discipline.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> The idea of treating bands as setup tools rather than continuous advice follows Bollinger&#39;s Official Rule 22. The exit examples in this lesson are used in this course and should be tested. <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Worked comparison: Decide what to do before each path appears</h4>\n<p>A long breakout plan enters after a defined close above 104, uses a structural invalidation below 102 and identifies a resistance area around 110. If price advances steadily toward 110, the preselected target or partial-exit rule controls the decision. If price falls through 102, the invalidation controls it. If price stalls at 107 without reaching either point, the written management rule—not impatience—decides whether to hold, reduce or exit.</p>\n<p>A poor plan says “I will see how it feels.” It can lead to widening stops after a loss or exiting too early because a candle looks intimidating. The middle band may be useful information, but it is not a universal exit for every method. A breakout, a range reversal and a squeeze-release trade have different reasons for entry. Match the management rule to that reason and apply it consistently while the outcome is still unknown.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Apply the plan: Choose the response in each path</h4>\n<p>For each path in Figure 7.2, state the action prescribed by the sample plan. Identify which action would change if you were testing a different setup.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> A target path leads to the planned exit; an invalidation path leads to exit or predefined risk reduction; an unresolved path follows the rule for managing no progress. Another setup may have a different target or management rule, but it should still be decided in advance.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>You planned a range trade to exit near the middle of the range, but price is still far from the target. You feel tempted to move the target farther away because the candle looks strong. What should you check?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> I should follow the rules I chose before entry, unless my plan explicitly allows a defined adjustment. A strong candle does not automatically justify changing the target. If I want to test a different management rule, I should evaluate it separately.</p>","quiz":{"question":"Why is the middle band not a universal exit rule?","choices":["Different setups have different reasons for entry and should use management rules matched to their tested premise.","The middle band never contains useful information.","It must always be ignored."],"correct":0,"why":"Use the middle band only if it belongs to the plan being tested. Predefine target, invalidation and how to handle unresolved paths."}},"7.3":{"title":"Test the Idea Without Fitting It to the Past","timeLabel":"16 minutes","take":"A useful test includes the trades that failed, the cases you skipped, and the conditions that were difficult—not just the wins.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 7.3</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>Does a setup work because it looks convincing on one chart?</p>\n<p>No. One good-looking example can explain an idea, but it cannot establish that the idea is reliable. You need to review how a clearly defined method behaves across different examples and conditions.</p>\n<p>You do not need a complex software system to begin. A small, consistent review table can help you discover whether you follow your own rules and where the idea appears to struggle.</p>\n<h4>Keep the rules stable during the review</h4>\n<p>Before reviewing examples, write down the method:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Which market context qualifies?</li>\n<li>What event triggers the entry?</li>\n<li>What confirms or cancels the idea?</li>\n<li>Where is the invalidation point?</li>\n<li>How is size calculated?</li>\n<li>What exit rule is used?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then evaluate each case using those same rules. Record a winner, a loser, a no-trade, or an ambiguous case honestly. Do not change the rules halfway through because a particular chart did not behave as you hoped.</p>\n<p>If you decide to test a new period length, band multiplier, confirmation or exit, call it a new version of the method. Review it separately under similar conditions. Repeatedly adjusting several settings until the past looks perfect is a form of overfitting. It can make a method look more reliable in the examples used to design it than it will be in new data.</p>\n<h4>Include different kinds of market conditions</h4>\n<p>A squeeze-breakout idea should not be evaluated only on strong breakouts. Include failed breaks, ranges, trends, and cases that never met the trigger. A range strategy should include cases where the range held and cases where it broke. An honest review includes no-trade cases, because those rules are part of the method.</p>\n<p>The results should not be reduced to win rate alone. Review whether the entry was late, whether the stop made sense, whether the planned reward justified risk, and whether the rules were followed. A high win rate can still hide poor losses, and a sound method can have losing trades.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Changing the method after every losing example until the history looks clean.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>A useful test includes the trades that failed, the cases you skipped, and the conditions that were difficult—not just the wins.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> Bollinger&#39;s Official Rules 9 and 11 caution against treating defaults or parameter choices as universal. The review process here is a method used in this course to test consistency, not a promise of future performance. <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules</a>.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Read Figure 7.3</h4>\n<p>The table includes several contexts. Look for the difference between reviewing many varied cases and selecting only the cases that make a setup look attractive. The second approach leaves important failure modes unseen.</p>\n<h4>Worked comparison: A fair review includes uncomfortable examples</h4>\n<p>Suppose you have a set of 40 candidate cases. Do not keep only the cleanest breakouts. Separate cases by context—trend, range, compression, failed first move and unclear structure—and record every case that met the stated rules, including those that stopped out or never offered a viable entry. This does not make 40 cases a guarantee of statistical certainty; it is a practical way to expose whether the rule only looks convincing in selected screenshots.</p>\n<p>If the rule performs differently in trending and ranging conditions, note the difference before changing it. Then test any proposed filter against cases that were not used to invent the filter. Keep entries, costs, stop logic and exit rules consistent during the first pass. If you change several inputs together, you will not know which change mattered. The aim is to learn where the method&#39;s conditions hold up, not to find a perfect-looking setting for the past.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Observe and interpret: Read the review table</h4>\n<p>Name two reasons the cherry-picked sample may give a misleading impression. Then state one change you would make to improve the review.</p>\n<p><strong>Model reasoning:</strong> The sample excludes difficult cases and may over-represent the exact conditions where the method looked best. A better review uses a consistent rule set and includes varied contexts, failures, and no-trade cases. The sample may still be too small to support a strong conclusion, and that limitation should be stated.</p>\n<h4>Test: Apply the reading to a new case</h4>\n<p>A modified band setting produces better results on the examples you just reviewed but performs worse on a different period. What should you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Model response:</strong> I should not declare the modification superior from the first sample. I need a consistent comparison and additional cases not used to tune the settings. I should also consider whether the rules are too fitted to a particular market condition.</p>","quiz":{"question":"What makes a review of a setup more credible?","choices":["Keep only the clearest winning screenshots.","Apply stable rules across varied conditions, including losses, failures and no-trade cases.","Change the parameters after every loss."],"correct":1,"why":"A varied sample reduces cherry-picking. Test proposed changes on cases that were not used to invent them."}},"7.4":{"title":"Review the Decision, Not Just the Result","timeLabel":"18 minutes","take":"Judge the process by the evidence and rules available at the decision point. The outcome is important feedback, but it is not the whole review.","ideaHtml":"<p>As you work through this lesson, refer to <strong>Figure 7.4</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The question</h4>\n<p>Can a losing trade follow a sound process, and can a winning trade be poorly planned?</p>\n<p>Yes. The result tells you what happened to that position. It does not, by itself, tell you whether the original decision was well reasoned.</p>\n<p>A trader can follow a defined plan, accept a planned loss, and still lose because the market did not develop as expected. Another trader can break the rules, take an impulsive entry, and make money by chance. If you judge only by profit and loss, the second trader may appear to have made the better decision. That is not a reliable way to improve.</p>\n<h4>Review what was knowable at the time</h4>\n<p>For each trade or no-trade decision, record:</p>\n<p><strong>Context:</strong> Was the market trending, ranging, compressed, or unclear? What visible evidence supported that reading?</p>\n<p><strong>Setup:</strong> Which framework did you choose, and why did it fit the context?</p>\n<p><strong>Trigger:</strong> Did the required event actually occur before entry?</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative:</strong> What else could the chart have meant? What evidence would have weakened your preferred explanation?</p>\n<p><strong>Invalidation and risk:</strong> Where was the idea wrong, how was the stop chosen, and was position size consistent with planned risk?</p>\n<p><strong>Management and result:</strong> Did you follow the planned exit rules? What happened afterward? Did the actual price path reveal a limitation in the plan?</p>\n<p><strong>Process note:</strong> Was the decision consistent with the rule set at the time, or did you change the plan under pressure?</p>\n<h4>Three types of review</h4>\n<p>In Figure 7.4, the disciplined losing trade has a clear setup, trigger, invalidation and controlled loss. The loss does not automatically prove the process was wrong. The undisciplined winning trade ignores a required condition but happens to profit. The no-trade case lacks a required piece of evidence, so waiting is consistent with the method.</p>\n<p>The examples are not a lesson in excusing every loss. If the trader made a calculation error, ignored a condition, or used an untested rule as if it were proven, that needs to be recorded. The point is to find the cause rather than use the outcome as the only verdict.</p>\n<h4>Assessment standard</h4>\n<p>A strong answer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>points to evidence in the figure;</li>\n<li>separates facts from interpretation;</li>\n<li>names a plausible alternative;</li>\n<li>describes what would strengthen or weaken the idea;</li>\n<li>defines invalidation and risk before the outcome;</li>\n<li>follows the chosen plan or explains why the plan has been cancelled;</li>\n<li>accepts “no trade” when the required conditions are absent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The assignment is not graded by whether you guessed the next price direction. It is about whether you made a defensible decision from the information available.</p>\n<h4>Common mistake</h4>\n<p>Calling the analysis good because a trade won, or bad simply because it lost.</p>\n<h4>Keep this</h4>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Judge the process by the evidence and rules available at the decision point. The outcome is important feedback, but it is not the whole review.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Source and further reading:</strong> This lesson synthesises the course&#39;s evidence-first approach. It is a TradeThesis assessment framework, not a Bollinger-prescribed grading system.</p>\n<hr>","examplesHtml":"<h4>Worked comparison: A loss can be disciplined; a win can be careless</h4>\n<p><strong>Case A — Disciplined loss.</strong> The defined setup appears, the entry follows the rule, the position is sized from the structural stop, and the trade exits at invalidation. The market then reverses. The result is negative, but the record shows a coherent decision made from the evidence available at entry.</p>\n<p><strong>Case B — Undisciplined win.</strong> The trader enters before confirmation, moves the stop farther away when price goes against the trade, and eventually exits at a profit after a sudden rally. The result is positive, but it does not show that the process was sound or repeatable.</p>\n<p><strong>Case C — No trade.</strong> A required condition is missing or the next obstacle leaves too little room. The trader passes and records why. Review each case without letting the final price path rewrite what could have been known at the decision point.</p>","practiceHtml":"<h4>Test: Complete the final assignment</h4>\n<p>Use the supplied cases to write:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>One complete long plan.</li>\n<li>One complete short plan.</li>\n<li>One no-trade decision.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For each, make your reasoning visible in three moves: <strong>observe</strong> the facts, <strong>interpret</strong> them while naming an alternative, and <strong>test</strong> the reading against the planned trigger, invalidation, or evidence you would wait for. Include the context, trigger, stop/size logic and exit rule where a trade is justified. If the chart does not offer enough evidence, say what is missing. Do not invent a trade merely to complete the assignment.</p>","quiz":{"question":"A trade loses after the written setup and risk rules were followed. How should the decision be reviewed?","choices":["The process must have been wrong because the result was negative.","The trade should be repeated with a larger size.","Assess the evidence and rule adherence separately from the outcome."],"correct":2,"why":"A single outcome cannot grade the full decision. Review what was knowable at entry and whether the plan was followed."}}};
